WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.074
Tired of searching for subtitles?
Ray generates them instantly: getray.app

2
00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:29.500
Michael:
<i>I was in my office at work</i>

3
00:00:29.584 --> 00:00:32.834
and I got a call
from an international number.

4
00:00:32.918 --> 00:00:34.709
Through the last
three years.

5
00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:37.584
when it was
an international call.
I'd answer it.

6
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.542
It was a reporter in Dublin

7
00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.292
who wanted a reaction
to the story.

8
00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:47.250
I said.
"Reaction for what?"

9
00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:50.792
They were really caught back
by that question. obviously.

10
00:00:50.876 --> 00:00:55.417
And they said.
"I'll call you back
in five minutes."

11
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.959
And so then I went online
and saw the picture.

12
00:01:06.334 --> 00:01:09.125
It's not the way
you want to find out.

13
00:01:09.209 --> 00:01:10.792
I never. ever imagined

14
00:01:10.876 --> 00:01:15.500
that it would end
in that fashion. right?

15
00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:21.459
<i>For days that first week.</i>

16
00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:24.542
<i>I just would constantly
see that image and...</i>

17
00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:29.459
<i>It's been tough.</i>

18
00:01:29.542 --> 00:01:32.667
<i>You go through
those emotional swings.</i>

19
00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:37.083
"Why the hell
did you go back
that second time?"

20
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.042
But I keep coming back
to the same answer.

21
00:02:08.626 --> 00:02:10.417
Host:
<i>Name the sports newspaper</i>

22
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.959
<i>that hit US newsstands
in 1990.</i>

23
00:02:13.042 --> 00:02:14.209
- ( buzzer sounds )
-<i> James?</i>

24
00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:16.250
- "The National."
-<i> It was.</i>

25
00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.918
Man:
<i>Ladies and gentlemen.</i>

26
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.292
<i>please help me welcome
Mr. James Foley.</i>

27
00:03:01.375 --> 00:03:04.667
<i>( applause )</i>

28
00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:10.375
Thanks for that generous--

29
00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:12.876
- overly generous...
-<i> ( laughter )</i>

30
00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.584
...introduction.

31
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.417
I'm definitely
not a hero

32
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.959
or noble or anything.

33
00:03:19.042 --> 00:03:22.542
I'm just trying
to do my work

34
00:03:22.626 --> 00:03:25.459
and got into
a little bit of trouble.

35
00:03:25.542 --> 00:03:30.250
<i>( men chanting )</i>

36
00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:33.918
<i>I arrived
in Benghazi in mid-March.</i>

37
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.209
<i>and the night I arrived.</i>

38
00:03:35.292 --> 00:03:37.584
<i>I heard nothing
but bombs and gunfire.</i>

39
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:39.459
<i>( men whooping )</i>

40
00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:41.292
<i>I wondered.
"What's going on?"</i>

41
00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:44.292
<i>Another journalist said.
"The bombs. that's gelatina.</i>

42
00:03:44.375 --> 00:03:46.542
That's how they fish."

43
00:03:46.626 --> 00:03:48.125
<i>( laughter )</i>

44
00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:49.709
Blow the fish up.

45
00:03:49.792 --> 00:03:51.209
"And the gunfire?

46
00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:52.918
No. that's
celebratory gunfire."

47
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.209
( gunfire )

48
00:03:56.292 --> 00:03:58.000
Jim:
<i>If Gaddafi</i>
<i>comes to Benghazi.</i>

49
00:03:58.083 --> 00:04:00.500
<i>are you guys
prepared to fight?</i>

50
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:16.876
Diane:
<i>I think in some ways</i>

51
00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.584
<i>Libya was
a turning point for Jim.</i>

52
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:21.375
I was starting to see

53
00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:23.292
his trying to figure out

54
00:04:23.375 --> 00:04:25.459
where he belonged
in the world.

55
00:04:25.542 --> 00:04:27.751
<i>He tried
the Teach for America.</i>

56
00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:32.083
<i>and then Chicago he worked
at the Cook County Jail.</i>

57
00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:34.292
<i>He anguished
over his teaching.</i>

58
00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:36.667
<i>He didn't think he was
a good enough teacher.</i>

59
00:04:36.751 --> 00:04:40.334
<i>He wanted to write
and he loved people</i>

60
00:04:40.417 --> 00:04:41.667
<i>and liked to interact.</i>

61
00:04:41.751 --> 00:04:43.042
<i>tell people's stories.</i>

62
00:04:43.125 --> 00:04:45.042
So when Jim decided
he wanted to go

63
00:04:45.125 --> 00:04:47.584
into journalism
from teaching.

64
00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:50.000
at first we thought
that would be good.

65
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:52.125
"Maybe that'll be
a better fit. Jim."

66
00:04:52.209 --> 00:04:56.709
But when he decided
to do conflict journalism.

67
00:04:56.792 --> 00:04:58.542
that was a whole
different deal.

68
00:04:58.626 --> 00:05:01.209
Well. he didn't
exactly tell us.

69
00:05:01.292 --> 00:05:02.292
( laughs )

70
00:05:02.375 --> 00:05:05.667
<i>He graduated from Medill</i>

71
00:05:05.751 --> 00:05:08.125
<i>and we said.
"Jim. what are you
going to do?"</i>

72
00:05:08.209 --> 00:05:10.042
And he said.
"Well. I'm working on it."

73
00:05:10.125 --> 00:05:13.876
The next thing we knew.
he was going to go to Iraq

74
00:05:13.959 --> 00:05:16.167
embedded with
the Indiana National Guard.

75
00:05:19.459 --> 00:05:23.125
<i>"Well. I guess
I'm a conflict journalist
at this point."</i>

76
00:05:23.209 --> 00:05:25.667
So that's how we found out.

77
00:05:25.751 --> 00:05:28.500
<i>( gunfire )</i>

78
00:05:28.584 --> 00:05:30.250
Jim:
<i>It's an all-out sprint.</i>

79
00:05:30.334 --> 00:05:32.876
<i>an exhilarating and dangerous
run for cover.</i>

80
00:05:32.959 --> 00:05:34.626
You got him?

81
00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.375
John Jr.:
<i>When Jim started</i>
<i>the journalism path.</i>

82
00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:42.918
we did have some heated
discussions on views

83
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.834
and the military
and whatnot.

84
00:05:44.918 --> 00:05:48.751
<i>My views are a little bit
more conservative than Jim's.</i>

85
00:05:48.834 --> 00:05:52.083
<i>I would say
a little bit more liberal.</i>

86
00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:53.918
<i>But when he did his embed.</i>

87
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.209
<i>I think. is where
we came closer together.</i>

88
00:05:58.292 --> 00:05:59.751
Jim:
<i>As soon as the shooting</i>
<i>died down.</i>

89
00:05:59.834 --> 00:06:02.834
<i>the platoon discovered
a body limp on the roof.</i>

90
00:06:05.626 --> 00:06:07.667
<i>Do they know
who the guy is?</i>

91
00:06:07.751 --> 00:06:09.792
Yes.

92
00:06:09.876 --> 00:06:11.083
His family members.

93
00:06:11.167 --> 00:06:12.792
-<i> These are the family?</i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

94
00:06:22.709 --> 00:06:25.918
John. Jr.:
<i>Then he came back to my house</i>
<i>and he told me--</i>

95
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.292
he was like.
"John. I'm thinking about
going to Libya."

96
00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:36.375
I'm like. "Jim.
that's a horrible idea.

97
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:40.667
<i>That is absolutely
a horrible idea.</i>

98
00:06:40.751 --> 00:06:45.959
If you go over there.
no one's coming for you.

99
00:06:46.042 --> 00:06:48.709
<i>Why would you put
your life in danger?</i>

100
00:06:48.792 --> 00:06:50.751
<i>We're dropping bombs
over there.</i>

101
00:06:50.834 --> 00:06:53.417
And God forbid
if you go over there

102
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.918
and we accidentally
kill you."

103
00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.959
I mean. why?

104
00:07:01.042 --> 00:07:03.167
Why?

105
00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:06.500
( chanting )

106
00:07:06.584 --> 00:07:08.375
Jim:
<i>Libya was very exciting</i>
<i>as a journalist</i>

107
00:07:08.459 --> 00:07:10.792
because you had this chance
to talk directly to people.

108
00:07:10.876 --> 00:07:12.042
to see exactly
what was going on.

109
00:07:12.125 --> 00:07:14.417
( chanting )

110
00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.000
<i>There were no
US soldiers anymore.</i>

111
00:07:16.083 --> 00:07:17.834
<i>No organized army
anymore telling you</i>

112
00:07:17.918 --> 00:07:19.042
<i>what you should
and shouldn't do.</i>

113
00:07:19.125 --> 00:07:20.417
( gunshots )

114
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.959
But it was also
extremely dangerous.

115
00:07:23.042 --> 00:07:25.626
- ( shouting )
- ( gunfire )

116
00:07:25.709 --> 00:07:27.667
I was actually talking
to one of my buddies

117
00:07:27.751 --> 00:07:29.459
boasting
about my brother Jim

118
00:07:29.542 --> 00:07:31.542
and how he's
this journalist

119
00:07:31.626 --> 00:07:34.959
and is super crazy.
but badass at the same time.

120
00:07:35.042 --> 00:07:37.626
<i>I was like. "Yeah.
my brother's a badass.</i>

121
00:07:37.709 --> 00:07:39.500
<i>He's crazy.
He's awesome."</i>

122
00:07:39.584 --> 00:07:41.250
I also was
very naive myself.

123
00:07:41.334 --> 00:07:42.876
I didn't know exactly
what that would mean.

124
00:07:42.959 --> 00:07:44.292
I didn't know
he was actually

125
00:07:44.375 --> 00:07:46.417
going to be immersed
in actual crossfire.

126
00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.334
-<i> ( gunfire )</i>
- There's snipers
in this building

127
00:07:48.417 --> 00:07:50.709
about one kilometer away.

128
00:07:50.792 --> 00:07:52.209
The group decided to go in.

129
00:07:52.292 --> 00:07:54.959
<i>Heavy fighting.</i>

130
00:07:55.042 --> 00:07:57.209
Heavy casualties.
About 30 wounded.

131
00:07:57.292 --> 00:08:00.167
two dead
as of this morning.

132
00:08:01.751 --> 00:08:03.042
Tom:
<i>There's one video</i>

133
00:08:03.125 --> 00:08:04.751
<i>that always
stands out to me.</i>

134
00:08:04.834 --> 00:08:08.209
It's when he's standing
in the square.

135
00:08:08.292 --> 00:08:11.000
There does seem to be
a strong sense

136
00:08:11.083 --> 00:08:12.667
that they won't
give up the fight.

137
00:08:12.751 --> 00:08:15.626
<i>And that they are a force
of young fighting men.</i>

138
00:08:15.709 --> 00:08:17.167
Although unorganized.

139
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:21.209
<i>there's plenty of will
to hold out here.</i>

140
00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:22.834
How does my guy
know this stuff?

141
00:08:22.918 --> 00:08:24.459
How does Jim Foley--

142
00:08:24.542 --> 00:08:27.459
<i>he's just my meathead
friend. you know?</i>

143
00:08:27.542 --> 00:08:32.125
It was so foreign to me
in terms of an experience.

144
00:08:32.209 --> 00:08:34.167
I'm like.
"How do you get into Libya?

145
00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.792
You're a freelancer?
Who wants this?

146
00:08:36.876 --> 00:08:38.709
<i>Are you getting paid?"</i>

147
00:08:38.792 --> 00:08:41.083
This is Jim Foley
reporting from downtown
Benghazi.

148
00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:43.959
Revolutionary Square.
"Global Post."

149
00:08:45.834 --> 00:08:49.042
Phil:
<i>Jim was there</i>
<i>at the early stage</i>

150
00:08:49.125 --> 00:08:50.542
of this movement
of there being

151
00:08:50.626 --> 00:08:53.751
more freelancers
in conflict areas.

152
00:08:55.375 --> 00:08:58.375
<i>The world
has changed so much</i>

153
00:08:58.459 --> 00:08:59.876
<i>in terms of
digital publishing</i>

154
00:08:59.959 --> 00:09:03.959
and newspapers started
to eliminate things

155
00:09:04.042 --> 00:09:06.667
that they didn't see
as essential.

156
00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:10.125
<i>International
coverage dwindled down
to very little.</i>

157
00:09:10.209 --> 00:09:13.626
<i>so we saw an opportunity
to fill that void</i>

158
00:09:13.709 --> 00:09:17.125
and we needed to work
with freelancers.

159
00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:19.292
Clare:
<i>Freelancers decide</i>
<i>to work together</i>

160
00:09:19.375 --> 00:09:24.417
just on the basis of this
initial quick-read chemistry.

161
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.751
<i>I saw this new guy
who I hadn't met before.</i>

162
00:09:28.834 --> 00:09:31.125
He looked friendly enough.
so I said. "Hey. what's up?"

163
00:09:31.209 --> 00:09:34.751
He said. "Not much.
Going to the frontline."

164
00:09:34.834 --> 00:09:37.500
<i>He's heard
a lot about Libya</i>

165
00:09:37.584 --> 00:09:40.667
<i>and the fact that
it was very cheap to work.</i>

166
00:09:40.751 --> 00:09:44.834
<i>Rebels and protestors
were eager to show us
their side of the story.</i>

167
00:09:44.918 --> 00:09:47.334
<i>They were driving us
all over for free.</i>

168
00:09:47.417 --> 00:09:50.959
<i>They were translating
for us for free.</i>

169
00:09:51.042 --> 00:09:54.209
Many of us never really
experienced the luxury

170
00:09:54.292 --> 00:09:56.667
of journalism
in its heyday.

171
00:09:56.751 --> 00:10:00.083
What we do is journalism
on a shoestring budget.

172
00:10:00.167 --> 00:10:04.209
<i>So we've had to be a lot
more resourceful in a way</i>

173
00:10:04.292 --> 00:10:06.667
<i>and just more street savvy.</i>

174
00:10:06.751 --> 00:10:10.375
I think. in a sense.
the way we all got
to know each other

175
00:10:10.459 --> 00:10:13.209
was the Africa Hotel
in Benghazi.

176
00:10:13.292 --> 00:10:16.334
<i>It was the cheapest hotel.
and the crappiest.</i>

177
00:10:16.417 --> 00:10:17.959
and we were all
staying there.

178
00:10:25.417 --> 00:10:28.542
I'd seen Jim talking
to a few other journalists.

179
00:10:28.626 --> 00:10:31.876
<i>and he was just really
friendly with everybody.</i>

180
00:10:31.959 --> 00:10:33.751
<i>It was unusual
in a place like that.</i>

181
00:10:33.834 --> 00:10:36.626
There's still an edge
of competitiveness
in that environment.

182
00:10:36.709 --> 00:10:39.626
whereas Jim was just like.
"Yeah. whatever."

183
00:10:39.709 --> 00:10:41.918
<i>He gave off a really good
first impression.</i>

184
00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.000
and it helps
that he's a super
good-looking guy.

185
00:10:45.083 --> 00:10:48.292
I was just like.
"Who is this guy?
Who are you?"

186
00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:55.792
<i>There wasn't anything mundane
about the man whatsoever.</i>

187
00:10:55.876 --> 00:10:58.667
He never really
projected himself
onto a situation

188
00:10:58.751 --> 00:11:02.667
<i>and he dealt
well with people.</i>

189
00:11:02.751 --> 00:11:04.834
Jim:
<i>Who are the Libyan rebels</i>

190
00:11:04.918 --> 00:11:07.292
<i>determined to overthrow
42 years of dictatorship?</i>

191
00:11:13.334 --> 00:11:14.751
-<i> Offshore welder.</i>
- Platform. yes.

192
00:11:14.834 --> 00:11:17.292
<i>Okay. Dangerous job.</i>

193
00:11:17.375 --> 00:11:18.792
<i>( laughs )</i>

194
00:11:18.876 --> 00:11:20.584
Jim:
<i>They're brave</i>
<i>as individuals.</i>

195
00:11:20.667 --> 00:11:23.584
<i>but many show a dangerous
lack of weapons training.</i>

196
00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:27.125
Clare:
<i>There were so many</i>
<i>freelancers</i>

197
00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:29.042
<i>who came in
at that point in time</i>

198
00:11:29.125 --> 00:11:31.959
<i>who were so new.
Jim and I among them.</i>

199
00:11:32.042 --> 00:11:35.417
and I found out only later
that there were a number

200
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.167
of much more experienced
photojournalists

201
00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.834
<i>who had made
one or two trips
to the frontline</i>

202
00:11:40.918 --> 00:11:43.751
and said. "Fuck it.
This is way too dangerous."

203
00:11:46.667 --> 00:11:48.792
<i>( Manu speaking )</i>

204
00:12:26.918 --> 00:12:28.834
Jim had a high tolerance
for danger.

205
00:12:28.918 --> 00:12:31.792
-<i> ( explosion )</i>
- Jim:<i> Shit.</i>

206
00:12:31.876 --> 00:12:35.417
Sure. he was drawn to that.
All of us are. in a way.

207
00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.083
He was cool as a cucumber
as well.

208
00:12:37.167 --> 00:12:41.334
<i>wearing this little
tiny vest he used to wear.</i>

209
00:12:41.417 --> 00:12:43.792
Clare:
<i>The fact that</i>
<i>he stayed so calm</i>

210
00:12:43.876 --> 00:12:48.000
<i>made it easy to feel calm
in that situation.</i>

211
00:12:48.083 --> 00:12:50.500
But. of course.
sometimes I was just like.

212
00:12:50.584 --> 00:12:52.792
"That's crazy.
I'm not going there with you."

213
00:12:56.042 --> 00:12:57.751
Jim:
<i>I would come back</i>
<i>to Benghazi</i>

214
00:12:57.834 --> 00:12:59.792
<i>and there was stuff
going on there.</i>

215
00:12:59.876 --> 00:13:01.542
<i>There was families</i>

216
00:13:01.626 --> 00:13:04.167
<i>and they were out there
in the protests.</i>

217
00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.125
<i>and they were out
there maybe handling
medical supplies.</i>

218
00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:09.292
which is probably
more important

219
00:13:09.375 --> 00:13:11.292
to what this
revolutionary movement was.

220
00:13:11.375 --> 00:13:14.834
<i>But then being called out
to that frontline again</i>

221
00:13:14.918 --> 00:13:18.751
<i>like some kind of
siren song...</i>

222
00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.542
<i>It was one of those mornings
where we decided</i>

223
00:13:24.626 --> 00:13:26.500
<i>we were gonna
get out there early.</i>

224
00:13:26.584 --> 00:13:28.125
<i>We wanted
to get a fresh look
at the frontlines.</i>

225
00:13:28.209 --> 00:13:30.375
<i>It was myself.
Clare Gillis.</i>

226
00:13:30.459 --> 00:13:32.751
<i>Manu Brabo.
and Anton Hammerl.</i>

227
00:13:32.834 --> 00:13:34.667
<i>a South African
photojournalist.</i>

228
00:13:34.751 --> 00:13:36.709
- Meet my friend!
- Anton.

229
00:13:36.792 --> 00:13:38.375
Anton!

230
00:13:38.459 --> 00:13:40.542
Jim:
<i>And what it was. really.</i>
<i>was just a highway.</i>

231
00:13:40.626 --> 00:13:42.751
<i>a coastal highway
going all across Libya.</i>

232
00:13:42.834 --> 00:13:45.125
<i>And this is where
the battle was going on.</i>

233
00:13:45.209 --> 00:13:48.667
<i>So it was kind of like
a Mad Max type war.</i>

234
00:13:50.626 --> 00:13:53.083
<i>This was something common
that some reporters did.</i>

235
00:13:53.167 --> 00:13:55.167
<i>Freelancers like myself
who didn't have big budgets.</i>

236
00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:57.500
<i>we'd jump in
with the rebels.</i>

237
00:13:57.584 --> 00:14:00.959
<i>It was at your own risk
if you wanted to go
further or not.</i>

238
00:14:03.209 --> 00:14:06.417
<i>We got to the point
where we saw another
group of rebels</i>

239
00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.751
<i>saying Gaddafi forces
are 300 meters away.</i>

240
00:14:09.834 --> 00:14:11.083
And myself.
looking at Clare.

241
00:14:11.167 --> 00:14:13.167
like.
"That's impossible."

242
00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:16.250
<i>I remember Anton
turning to me</i>

243
00:14:16.334 --> 00:14:18.918
<i>and saying.
"Hey. this isn't safe."</i>

244
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.375
<i>But we didn't turn around.</i>

245
00:14:22.459 --> 00:14:25.834
<i>We said. "Let's get off
the road anyways."</i>

246
00:14:25.918 --> 00:14:27.918
That was the exact
wrong thing to do.

247
00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.500
<i>Two heavily armed
Gaddafi pickup trucks</i>

248
00:14:32.584 --> 00:14:35.125
<i>came over
that rise firing.</i>

249
00:14:37.375 --> 00:14:38.667
<i>I remember so clearly</i>

250
00:14:38.751 --> 00:14:41.459
<i>the sound of it.
the volume of it.</i>

251
00:14:41.542 --> 00:14:45.459
<i>the sound of something
heating metal.</i>

252
00:14:45.542 --> 00:14:48.000
<i>And I remember
hoping against hope</i>

253
00:14:48.083 --> 00:14:50.918
<i>that there would be
some kind of out
out of this.</i>

254
00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.375
<i>there was some kind
of trapdoor in time.</i>

255
00:14:58.334 --> 00:15:01.083
<i>I crawled back
to the sand dune.</i>

256
00:15:01.167 --> 00:15:03.375
<i>Anton was at
the other sand dune
in front of me.</i>

257
00:15:03.459 --> 00:15:05.250
<i>I heard him call for help.</i>

258
00:15:07.083 --> 00:15:10.083
<i>It appeared he was cut across
the midsection with AK fire.</i>

259
00:15:10.167 --> 00:15:12.125
<i>and there was
a serious amount of blood.</i>

260
00:15:12.209 --> 00:15:13.584
<i>He had already
lost consciousness</i>

261
00:15:13.667 --> 00:15:15.209
<i>and probably already died.</i>

262
00:15:20.083 --> 00:15:22.626
<i>A group of young soldiers
approached me</i>

263
00:15:22.709 --> 00:15:25.542
<i>and we were thrown
in the back of a truck.</i>

264
00:15:25.626 --> 00:15:29.459
I remember
getting photographed
with a cell phone

265
00:15:29.542 --> 00:15:33.500
<i>and thinking
this is where they find
all these photographs</i>

266
00:15:33.584 --> 00:15:36.584
<i>that are evidence
of war crimes someday.</i>

267
00:15:36.667 --> 00:15:39.083
<i>and realizing
this is me now.</i>

268
00:15:47.626 --> 00:15:49.250
Diane:
<i>I was with my mother.</i>

269
00:15:49.334 --> 00:15:51.876
<i>We were out to lunch
and I received a phone call.</i>

270
00:15:51.959 --> 00:15:56.918
And that's how
we first heard. you know?

271
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.792
I think I was in denial
about how dangerous

272
00:15:59.876 --> 00:16:03.876
this really was. Brian.

273
00:16:03.959 --> 00:16:07.083
I was furious.

274
00:16:07.167 --> 00:16:09.083
just furious.

275
00:16:09.167 --> 00:16:11.375
Scared for him.
furious.

276
00:16:11.459 --> 00:16:13.709
I hate to revisit
it. but it was like.

277
00:16:13.792 --> 00:16:16.542
"I told you. Jim."

278
00:16:16.626 --> 00:16:20.709
I think we all
went through the stages
of total shock

279
00:16:20.792 --> 00:16:25.125
and then just.
"What are we going to do?"

280
00:16:25.209 --> 00:16:27.000
And then anger.

281
00:16:27.083 --> 00:16:29.500
After all.
we're family. you know?

282
00:16:32.918 --> 00:16:34.667
Jim:
<i>You're so humbled.</i>

283
00:16:34.751 --> 00:16:36.542
<i>You lost everything--</i>

284
00:16:36.626 --> 00:16:39.334
<i>your freedom.
your control.</i>

285
00:16:39.417 --> 00:16:41.167
<i>your ability
to talk to anybody</i>

286
00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.584
<i>and tell anybody
you're okay.</i>

287
00:16:42.667 --> 00:16:44.417
<i>Thinking one minute.
"Oh. yeah.</i>

288
00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.334
<i>I'm a foreign
correspondent."</i>

289
00:16:46.417 --> 00:16:50.083
<i>The next minute.
somebody who you
respect killed</i>

290
00:16:50.167 --> 00:16:52.709
<i>and you have nothing.</i>

291
00:16:56.709 --> 00:16:59.250
Clare:
<i>Jim was concerned</i>

292
00:16:59.334 --> 00:17:02.167
<i>that his own
competitiveness</i>

293
00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:04.834
with Manu. with Anton.
with himself.

294
00:17:04.918 --> 00:17:08.375
his own sort of
macho aggressiveness

295
00:17:08.459 --> 00:17:10.792
had driven him
to make decisions

296
00:17:10.876 --> 00:17:12.417
that were not
the best decisions.

297
00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:15.167
<i>We were all questioning
our judgment.</i>

298
00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:18.542
<i>One of the main things
that affected all of us</i>

299
00:17:18.626 --> 00:17:24.459
<i>is that Anton had kids
and none of us do.</i>

300
00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:26.918
Jim:
<i>Every day I have to</i>
<i>deal with the fact</i>

301
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:31.292
<i>that Anton is not
going to ever see
his three kids anymore.</i>

302
00:17:31.375 --> 00:17:33.959
and I was part of that
decision-making process...

303
00:17:36.626 --> 00:17:38.000
that took him away--

304
00:17:38.083 --> 00:17:40.542
that took him away
from his kids and his wife.

305
00:17:44.876 --> 00:17:47.042
<i>( Manu speaking )</i>

306
00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.042
Jim:
<i>I had a lot of time</i>
<i>to play over those moments.</i>

307
00:17:59.125 --> 00:18:02.125
<i>especially that one day
when we were captured.</i>

308
00:18:02.209 --> 00:18:04.667
<i>I tried to question myself.
"What are you reporting on?</i>

309
00:18:04.751 --> 00:18:07.000
<i>What is this all about?"</i>

310
00:18:07.083 --> 00:18:08.667
<i>It was quickly apparent</i>

311
00:18:08.751 --> 00:18:11.334
<i>that this was about
being what you think</i>

312
00:18:11.417 --> 00:18:13.626
<i>is an authentic
conflict correspondent.</i>

313
00:18:13.709 --> 00:18:16.125
<i>seeing the frontline.
and it not being enough</i>

314
00:18:16.209 --> 00:18:18.334
<i>to just see it
from a distance.</i>

315
00:18:18.417 --> 00:18:20.667
<i>but to push it
to the next level.</i>

316
00:18:20.751 --> 00:18:23.000
<i>You were basically
waiting to get shelled.</i>

317
00:18:23.083 --> 00:18:26.334
and the question is.
"Why?"

318
00:18:26.417 --> 00:18:28.334
You know.
"Why are you doing this?"

319
00:18:32.292 --> 00:18:34.417
Michael:
<i>A lot of us were just</i>
<i>scratching our head. right?</i>

320
00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:37.125
<i>There's no money.
Maybe you get a story</i>

321
00:18:37.209 --> 00:18:38.792
<i>here and there
that you sell.</i>

322
00:18:38.876 --> 00:18:40.918
I used to loan him money.

323
00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.292
<i>My credit report
has one ding on it.</i>

324
00:18:44.375 --> 00:18:47.792
and it's the one loan
that I cosigned with Jim.

325
00:18:47.876 --> 00:18:53.250
I think Jimmy was just
a little outside the lines.

326
00:18:53.334 --> 00:18:55.292
He was wicked disorganized.

327
00:18:55.375 --> 00:18:57.083
Mark:
<i>We had to tell him</i>
<i>to come to dinner.</i>

328
00:18:57.167 --> 00:19:00.209
If dinner was at 5:00.
we'd tell him it was at 3:00.

329
00:19:00.292 --> 00:19:02.042
so he'd show up at 5:00.

330
00:19:02.125 --> 00:19:03.959
He always lives
in the moment.

331
00:19:04.042 --> 00:19:05.542
<i>I don't know
if Mike ever told you</i>

332
00:19:05.626 --> 00:19:08.000
<i>that Jim was late
to his wedding.</i>

333
00:19:08.083 --> 00:19:10.167
"John. can I sleep
at your house?

334
00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:12.792
<i>John. can my friend
stay at your house?"</i>

335
00:19:12.876 --> 00:19:16.250
<i>"Yes. Jim. yes."</i>

336
00:19:16.334 --> 00:19:19.500
Even though you were like.
"Jim. just wake up!

337
00:19:19.584 --> 00:19:21.542
Wake up!
What are you doing?

338
00:19:21.626 --> 00:19:22.834
<i>Get a real job.</i>

339
00:19:22.918 --> 00:19:25.876
<i>Start saving up
for retirement."</i>

340
00:19:25.959 --> 00:19:28.626
I remember I said. "Why don't
you come to Chicago?

341
00:19:28.709 --> 00:19:30.584
<i>My dad runs this boot camp</i>

342
00:19:30.667 --> 00:19:32.834
<i>that's teaching
young felons."</i>

343
00:19:32.918 --> 00:19:34.584
<i>He goes there
for the interview
and I call him afterwards</i>

344
00:19:34.667 --> 00:19:36.459
and I'm like.
"How'd the interview go?"

345
00:19:36.542 --> 00:19:39.584
He's like. "It was great.
I think it went great."

346
00:19:39.667 --> 00:19:41.375
<i>I call my dad and he goes.</i>

347
00:19:41.459 --> 00:19:42.959
"He said it went great.
did he?

348
00:19:43.042 --> 00:19:45.334
Because I just talked to
the lady at the hiring board.

349
00:19:45.417 --> 00:19:48.209
and when she went out there.
she found Jim sleeping

350
00:19:48.292 --> 00:19:50.209
<i>with his head
back on the wall</i>

351
00:19:50.292 --> 00:19:51.751
<i>and he's wearing jeans
with paint or something
all over them."</i>

352
00:19:51.834 --> 00:19:53.584
I call Jim back.
I'm like. "Jim.

353
00:19:53.667 --> 00:19:55.542
what the hell happened
at the interview?"

354
00:19:55.626 --> 00:19:57.292
He's like.
"It was hot in there. Tom.

355
00:19:57.375 --> 00:19:59.709
It was hot.
I just put my head back
and I nodded off."

356
00:19:59.792 --> 00:20:01.792
I'm like.
"Did you have jeans on
with paint?"

357
00:20:01.876 --> 00:20:04.042
<i>And he's like.
"Yeah. they had these
symbols on them.</i>

358
00:20:04.125 --> 00:20:06.375
What was I supposed to wear?"
I'm like. "Jim. it's
an interview.

359
00:20:06.459 --> 00:20:08.375
It's an interview.
You can't do this."

360
00:20:08.459 --> 00:20:11.417
<i>So my dad
always says that Jim had
a million-dollar resume</i>

361
00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:14.125
and a 10-cent interview.

362
00:20:14.209 --> 00:20:16.918
<i>He came
and he started teaching
reading and writing.</i>

363
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.292
<i>some literature.
and he was really good
with these kids.</i>

364
00:20:20.375 --> 00:20:22.459
because Jim was into
hip-hop and rap

365
00:20:22.542 --> 00:20:24.626
<i>and he liked
to grab a mic himself.</i>

366
00:20:24.709 --> 00:20:26.375
Jim connected
with these guys.

367
00:20:26.459 --> 00:20:28.626
He'd find out
about their backgrounds.
talk to them.

368
00:20:28.709 --> 00:20:30.334
I think that
really hit him.

369
00:20:30.417 --> 00:20:31.918
<i>He was seeing young guys</i>

370
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.959
<i>that came from
terrible neighborhoods</i>

371
00:20:34.042 --> 00:20:35.584
<i>that never really
had a chance.</i>

372
00:20:35.667 --> 00:20:37.209
<i>I think he started seeing</i>

373
00:20:37.292 --> 00:20:39.417
<i>there are people who need
their stories told.</i>

374
00:20:43.709 --> 00:20:46.584
Jim:
<i>The prison that we were</i>
<i>transferred to</i>

375
00:20:46.667 --> 00:20:50.375
<i>was filled with all
these political prisoners
from all walks of life.</i>

376
00:20:50.459 --> 00:20:52.584
<i>Some were fishermen.
some were engineers.</i>

377
00:20:52.667 --> 00:20:54.959
<i>It was the warmth
of these Libyans that said.</i>

378
00:20:55.042 --> 00:20:56.792
"Here you're our guest.
Take this bed.

379
00:20:56.876 --> 00:20:58.500
You don't have a cigarette?
Here's a cigarette."

380
00:20:58.584 --> 00:21:00.125
"You want an extra piece
of chicken? Here."

381
00:21:02.167 --> 00:21:04.250
<i>These guys understand
that I'm a journalist</i>

382
00:21:04.334 --> 00:21:06.000
<i>and I'm trying
to get the truth.</i>

383
00:21:06.083 --> 00:21:07.876
and perhaps wrongly
they think I'm on their side.

384
00:21:07.959 --> 00:21:10.000
but I certainly
feel like it now.

385
00:21:10.083 --> 00:21:12.459
<i>( chanting over P.A. )</i>

386
00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:19.292
<i>You know that if you
are kind of without hope.</i>

387
00:21:19.375 --> 00:21:20.959
<i>you're totally humbled.</i>

388
00:21:21.042 --> 00:21:24.209
<i>you tend to go to your faith
if you have faith.</i>

389
00:21:24.292 --> 00:21:26.918
<i>Praying five times a day--
it was the life.</i>

390
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:29.751
<i>It was their only source
of life in prison.</i>

391
00:21:29.834 --> 00:21:32.000
<i>I was up nights
talking to them.</i>

392
00:21:32.083 --> 00:21:34.709
<i>Eventually. one of them said.
"Why don't you pray with us?"</i>

393
00:21:34.792 --> 00:21:36.709
And I said.
"No. I'll pray to Isa.

394
00:21:36.792 --> 00:21:38.334
I pray to Jesus
when you guys are praying."

395
00:21:38.417 --> 00:21:41.626
He sort of looked at me
like. "Okay."

396
00:21:41.709 --> 00:21:43.918
<i>I guess
I'm an experiential person.</i>

397
00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.626
<i>so I started
praying with them.</i>

398
00:21:49.667 --> 00:21:53.042
Little did I know.
I just proclaimed my
conversion to Islam.

399
00:21:53.125 --> 00:21:54.834
( laughter )

400
00:21:54.918 --> 00:21:58.834
So from then on out.
I prayed with them
five times a day.

401
00:21:58.918 --> 00:22:01.959
<i>And it was just--
it was so powerful.</i>

402
00:22:02.042 --> 00:22:04.542
<i>and it was something
that I needed to do.</i>

403
00:22:04.626 --> 00:22:06.042
<i>But it was difficult.</i>

404
00:22:06.125 --> 00:22:08.709
I was thinking.
"Jesus. am I praying to Allah?

405
00:22:08.792 --> 00:22:12.083
Am I violating
my belief in you?"

406
00:22:12.167 --> 00:22:14.167
<i>I don't have
an answer to that.</i>

407
00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:16.459
<i>I just know
that I was authentically
praying with them</i>

408
00:22:16.542 --> 00:22:19.125
<i>and I was authentically
also praying to Jesus.</i>

409
00:22:23.459 --> 00:22:26.500
Michael:
<i>Jim and I were</i>
<i>very close in age.</i>

410
00:22:26.584 --> 00:22:30.209
<i>look enough alike that
I used his ID in college.</i>

411
00:22:30.292 --> 00:22:31.584
<i>but that's about it.</i>

412
00:22:31.667 --> 00:22:33.334
<i>We couldn't be
more different.</i>

413
00:22:33.417 --> 00:22:36.834
Jim was really different
from most of us. right?

414
00:22:36.918 --> 00:22:39.167
<i>I mean. to do
what he went on to do.</i>

415
00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.709
<i>it's not a normal path.</i>

416
00:22:41.792 --> 00:22:43.876
<i>When Jim was taken
in Libya.</i>

417
00:22:43.959 --> 00:22:46.667
<i>I just went into
overdrive at that point.</i>

418
00:22:46.751 --> 00:22:49.334
It's a nightmare.
Each day it becomes harder

419
00:22:49.417 --> 00:22:51.209
with the lack
of information.

420
00:22:51.292 --> 00:22:52.918
We know he's in Tripoli.

421
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:54.667
<i>We believe he's in
a detention center.</i>

422
00:22:54.751 --> 00:22:58.334
<i>We really don't know
much more beyond that.</i>

423
00:22:58.417 --> 00:23:01.375
We organized this huge group
of Jimmy's friends.

424
00:23:01.459 --> 00:23:03.417
We called them FOJs--
friends of Jim.

425
00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.125
<i>We had a lot
of outside help.</i>

426
00:23:05.209 --> 00:23:07.042
<i>but Michael was sort of
the CEO of the group.</i>

427
00:23:07.125 --> 00:23:09.792
His statement was
there are no measurables

428
00:23:09.876 --> 00:23:11.709
other than getting
Jim home.

429
00:23:11.792 --> 00:23:15.626
Our biggest fear
is that it becomes
yesterday's story

430
00:23:15.709 --> 00:23:17.500
<i>and people
forget about it.</i>

431
00:23:17.584 --> 00:23:19.667
<i>We love Jim
and we miss him.</i>

432
00:23:19.751 --> 00:23:21.667
<i>We want him home.</i>

433
00:23:23.792 --> 00:23:25.334
Brian:
<i>Tell me about the release.</i>

434
00:23:25.417 --> 00:23:27.459
<i>What was that day like
for you guys?</i>

435
00:23:29.584 --> 00:23:31.792
Reporter:
<i>After successful</i>
<i>diplomatic negotiations.</i>

436
00:23:31.876 --> 00:23:35.250
<i>two American journalists
and a Spanish journalist
are finally going home</i>

437
00:23:35.334 --> 00:23:36.959
<i>after being kidnapped
and detained</i>

438
00:23:37.042 --> 00:23:38.918
<i>by the Libyan government
forces for 44 days.</i>

439
00:23:39.042 --> 00:23:40.834
- What is your name?
- Manu Brabo.

440
00:23:40.918 --> 00:23:43.042
- Were you okay?
Were you looked after?
- I'm fine.

441
00:23:43.125 --> 00:23:45.709
If you didn't catch
it the first time around.

442
00:23:45.792 --> 00:23:47.250
get it from your buddy.

443
00:23:47.334 --> 00:23:49.918
Guys. could you please
give them some space?

444
00:23:51.959 --> 00:23:53.959
Michael:
<i>What was really interesting</i>
<i>about that whole process.</i>

445
00:23:54.042 --> 00:23:56.834
there was a day
where we were making
some progress

446
00:23:56.918 --> 00:23:59.167
and I just decided
that I have to go.

447
00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:00.751
I have to get on
a plane and go.

448
00:24:00.834 --> 00:24:03.167
<i>I don't know.
I can't describe it.</i>

449
00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.792
<i>But I knew--
at least felt strongly--</i>

450
00:24:05.876 --> 00:24:09.250
<i>that it was going to
work out.</i>

451
00:24:09.334 --> 00:24:11.959
<i>All the security guys
wanted to debrief him first.</i>

452
00:24:12.042 --> 00:24:13.334
<i>and finally I said.
"Enough of this shit"</i>

453
00:24:13.417 --> 00:24:15.584
and just went into
the hotel room.

454
00:24:15.667 --> 00:24:18.167
I remember seeing Jim.
he had a full beard.

455
00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:21.626
and he just grabbed me
and said. "Mike."

456
00:24:21.709 --> 00:24:24.500
<i>We both kind of just
grabbed each other's arms.</i>

457
00:24:24.584 --> 00:24:26.792
<i>like. just to--
is it real?</i>

458
00:24:26.876 --> 00:24:28.250
<i>I ordered pizza</i>

459
00:24:28.334 --> 00:24:30.000
and as many Heinekens
as they could put in a car

460
00:24:30.083 --> 00:24:31.751
and about two packs
of cigarettes

461
00:24:31.834 --> 00:24:33.459
<i>because I knew that's all
they had over there.</i>

462
00:24:33.542 --> 00:24:38.042
I looked at them and I said.
"I guess I got this one."

463
00:24:38.125 --> 00:24:39.792
<i>and we just stayed up
all night.</i>

464
00:24:43.042 --> 00:24:44.542
Katie:
<i>All the family</i>
<i>and friends.</i>

465
00:24:44.626 --> 00:24:47.000
<i>we're all together
waiting for them.</i>

466
00:24:49.417 --> 00:24:50.959
<i>And all of a sudden we see</i>

467
00:24:51.042 --> 00:24:54.334
<i>these blue lights
just rushing at us.</i>

468
00:24:54.417 --> 00:24:55.792
( sirens blaring )

469
00:24:55.876 --> 00:24:57.459
We're like.
"Oh. my God. this is Jim.

470
00:24:57.542 --> 00:24:59.834
This is--
he's actually here."

471
00:24:59.918 --> 00:25:01.834
<i>I don't know.
it was like a movie.</i>

472
00:25:01.918 --> 00:25:04.626
<i>He was so happy
to see everybody.</i>

473
00:25:19.834 --> 00:25:21.834
Jim:
<i>You have a close call.</i>

474
00:25:21.918 --> 00:25:23.834
<i>That's pure luck
that you didn't get
killed there.</i>

475
00:25:23.918 --> 00:25:27.667
<i>It's not worth seeing your
mother or father bawling</i>

476
00:25:27.751 --> 00:25:30.334
<i>and worrying about
your grandmother dying</i>

477
00:25:30.417 --> 00:25:31.584
<i>because you're in prison.</i>

478
00:25:31.667 --> 00:25:33.500
<i>It's not worth these things.</i>

479
00:25:35.792 --> 00:25:37.876
<i>And outside.
in my parents' home</i>

480
00:25:37.959 --> 00:25:40.042
in a comfortable house
in New Hampshire.

481
00:25:40.125 --> 00:25:42.375
I sort of had
to start processing.

482
00:25:42.459 --> 00:25:44.250
<i>I was horrified to learn</i>

483
00:25:44.334 --> 00:25:46.167
<i>how much
my friends and family
had done to help me.</i>

484
00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:47.792
<i>I was inspired
and I was horrified.</i>

485
00:25:47.876 --> 00:25:51.500
It was a weird feeling of.
like. going to your own funeral.

486
00:25:54.626 --> 00:25:57.083
<i>There's no going back
from something like this.</i>

487
00:25:59.542 --> 00:26:01.125
<i>Some of the things
I'll never be able to change.</i>

488
00:26:01.209 --> 00:26:03.459
<i>but I wish--
I wish that I could.</i>

489
00:26:13.209 --> 00:26:14.834
- This is--
- What are you gonna do?

490
00:26:14.918 --> 00:26:16.834
This is Jim's blazer from
when he spoke at Marquette.

491
00:26:16.918 --> 00:26:18.000
this brown one.

492
00:26:20.459 --> 00:26:22.709
Bunch of stuff
to go through.

493
00:26:24.334 --> 00:26:27.375
Yeah. so Jim lived with us
for. what. three months?

494
00:26:27.459 --> 00:26:30.209
Summer after he was
released from Libya.

495
00:26:30.292 --> 00:26:34.417
Summer until-- yeah.
up until he went back.

496
00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:36.209
Used to have all
his clothes in here.

497
00:26:36.292 --> 00:26:39.626
You can still see
his CamelBak from
the field.

498
00:26:39.709 --> 00:26:42.334
-<i> Sleeping bags.</i>
- All his sleeping bags.

499
00:26:42.417 --> 00:26:44.167
Yeah.

500
00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:45.834
That was his bed
right there.

501
00:26:45.918 --> 00:26:49.375
He'd always
crash out there.

502
00:26:49.459 --> 00:26:51.167
<i>So it was cool
having Jim here.</i>

503
00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.375
especially after him
having been gone in Libya.

504
00:26:53.459 --> 00:26:56.918
because I think
when he came back.

505
00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.500
you just wanted
to touch him a lot.

506
00:26:59.584 --> 00:27:02.626
<i>Because. I don't know.
for me it was like. poke.</i>

507
00:27:02.709 --> 00:27:04.042
<i>and just make sure
he's real.</i>

508
00:27:04.125 --> 00:27:05.542
<i>It was almost--</i>

509
00:27:05.626 --> 00:27:07.334
<i>you're just
more appreciative of him.</i>

510
00:27:07.417 --> 00:27:10.125
especially with the kids
getting to have him here

511
00:27:10.209 --> 00:27:13.000
<i>because he was
always on the go.</i>

512
00:27:13.083 --> 00:27:15.125
<i>They loved their Uncle Jim.</i>

513
00:27:15.209 --> 00:27:18.459
<i>They'd come downstairs
at the crack of dawn
and wake him up.</i>

514
00:27:18.542 --> 00:27:21.709
<i>It was good
as much as it--</i>

515
00:27:21.792 --> 00:27:23.918
I think he had an itch
he couldn't scratch

516
00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.751
<i>when he was domesticated.</i>

517
00:27:30.042 --> 00:27:31.834
Phil:
<i>When Jim came back</i>
<i>from Libya.</i>

518
00:27:31.918 --> 00:27:35.792
I offered him
a full-time job here
as an editor.

519
00:27:35.876 --> 00:27:40.834
while he sorted out
what he would do next.

520
00:27:40.918 --> 00:27:45.250
He sat right outside
my office.

521
00:27:45.334 --> 00:27:47.667
<i>He was grateful
to have the job.</i>

522
00:27:47.751 --> 00:27:50.417
<i>but working in an office</i>

523
00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:55.125
was clearly not
what he liked the most.

524
00:27:55.209 --> 00:27:58.250
<i>He was quiet.</i>

525
00:27:58.334 --> 00:28:01.167
<i>I don't want to say
he was withdrawn.</i>

526
00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.250
<i>but he was quiet.</i>

527
00:28:05.167 --> 00:28:07.042
Katie:
<i>And I just remember Brad--</i>

528
00:28:07.125 --> 00:28:09.876
<i>my fiancé-- and Jim
were in the basement.</i>

529
00:28:09.959 --> 00:28:13.209
and Brad doesn't
even remember a time

530
00:28:13.292 --> 00:28:14.792
where Jim was sleeping.

531
00:28:14.876 --> 00:28:17.792
<i>After the family
had kind of settled down.</i>

532
00:28:17.876 --> 00:28:20.751
<i>Jim just went
right to business.
right to work.</i>

533
00:28:20.834 --> 00:28:25.375
Diane:
<i>We had found him a very good</i>
<i>psychologist to talk to.</i>

534
00:28:25.459 --> 00:28:28.334
but he seemed so well.
if you will.

535
00:28:28.417 --> 00:28:30.292
that we didn't push it.

536
00:28:30.375 --> 00:28:32.834
But he was so restless
here at home.

537
00:28:32.918 --> 00:28:34.626
<i>He didn't want to
be here at home.</i>

538
00:28:38.209 --> 00:28:40.125
Jim:
<i>Feeling like you</i>
<i>survived something.</i>

539
00:28:40.209 --> 00:28:42.125
<i>it has a strange
sort of force</i>

540
00:28:42.209 --> 00:28:45.167
<i>that you are
drawn back to.</i>

541
00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:47.542
I think that's
the absolute reality.

542
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.167
Zac:
<i>Everything else</i>
<i>becomes foreign.</i>

543
00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.292
It was strange coming back
to the real world.

544
00:28:57.375 --> 00:29:01.000
It was weird.
It was normal in there.

545
00:29:01.083 --> 00:29:03.626
Clare:
<i>Yeah. if I go to Wal-Mart</i>
<i>and I'm looking at</i>

546
00:29:03.709 --> 00:29:07.918
<i>literally an entire aisle
full of Tupperware.</i>

547
00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:09.459
it boggles my mind.

548
00:29:09.542 --> 00:29:13.042
<i>There's a deep absurdity</i>

549
00:29:13.125 --> 00:29:17.042
to understanding the peace

550
00:29:17.125 --> 00:29:20.167
<i>that we're lucky enough
to have in this country</i>

551
00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:22.626
<i>and the questions
that it raises</i>

552
00:29:22.709 --> 00:29:25.167
<i>about how do
we make our money.</i>

553
00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:27.542
<i>what is the basis
of our peace.</i>

554
00:29:27.626 --> 00:29:29.876
<i>of our economic viability.</i>

555
00:29:29.959 --> 00:29:33.584
<i>of the fact that I can look
at all of this Tupperware</i>

556
00:29:33.667 --> 00:29:36.292
<i>and someone else
somewhere else</i>

557
00:29:36.375 --> 00:29:38.626
<i>is looking at
the ruins of their home.</i>

558
00:29:40.959 --> 00:29:42.459
Zac:
<i>You start missing</i>
<i>all the bombs</i>

559
00:29:42.542 --> 00:29:44.417
<i>and the fighting
and everything.</i>

560
00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.167
<i>I just used to love
sitting up at night</i>

561
00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:47.959
<i>and just listening.</i>

562
00:29:48.042 --> 00:29:50.209
<i>( gunfire )</i>

563
00:29:50.292 --> 00:29:51.834
It's really kind of sick.

564
00:29:51.918 --> 00:29:54.167
- ( explosions )
-<i> But it also--</i>

565
00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:57.167
<i>it feels super fortunate
for being there.</i>

566
00:29:57.250 --> 00:30:00.375
<i>You think. like.
out of 25 million people
in your country.</i>

567
00:30:00.459 --> 00:30:03.500
you're the only person
who's got to experience that.

568
00:30:03.584 --> 00:30:05.876
Like. fuck. man.
how fortunate am I?

569
00:30:05.959 --> 00:30:07.584
<i>You're witnessing
history unfolding</i>

570
00:30:07.667 --> 00:30:10.709
<i>and you're getting
this perspective</i>

571
00:30:10.792 --> 00:30:13.292
<i>that is so unique.</i>

572
00:30:14.792 --> 00:30:17.250
<i>( Manu speaking )</i>

573
00:31:06.292 --> 00:31:09.417
I believe that frontline
journalism is important.

574
00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:12.083
Without these photos
and videos

575
00:31:12.167 --> 00:31:14.042
and firsthand experience.

576
00:31:14.125 --> 00:31:17.417
we can't really
tell the world

577
00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:19.584
<i>how bad it might be.</i>

578
00:31:19.667 --> 00:31:21.792
( singing in Arabic )

579
00:31:25.709 --> 00:31:28.000
( explosion )

580
00:31:34.542 --> 00:31:36.334
Woman:
<i>James Foley</i>
<i>joins us now live</i>

581
00:31:36.417 --> 00:31:38.334
<i>from inside northern Syria</i>

582
00:31:38.417 --> 00:31:40.375
<i>with more on what he saw.</i>

583
00:31:40.459 --> 00:31:43.584
<i>Tell us more about what
you were able to witness.</i>

584
00:31:43.667 --> 00:31:45.083
Yes. thank you.

585
00:31:45.167 --> 00:31:47.083
You've heard about
indiscriminate shelling.

586
00:31:47.167 --> 00:31:49.792
but to see
those bodies left over

587
00:31:49.876 --> 00:31:53.083
<i>from a direct mortar hit
was really shocking.</i>

588
00:31:53.167 --> 00:31:54.667
<i>These were civilians.</i>

589
00:31:54.751 --> 00:31:57.542
<i>and they are under pretty
continuous shelling.</i>

590
00:31:57.626 --> 00:31:59.792
( chanting )

591
00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:13.626
Thank you.

592
00:32:17.083 --> 00:32:21.959
It seemed like
he started thinking
about going to Syria.

593
00:32:22.042 --> 00:32:24.626
and by the time
he mentioned it.

594
00:32:24.709 --> 00:32:27.918
<i>it was like he'd already
kind of made up his mind.</i>

595
00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:31.834
And he said he was going
with John Cantlie.

596
00:32:31.918 --> 00:32:34.876
who's another colleague--
a British photojournalist

597
00:32:34.959 --> 00:32:38.709
<i>that we'd all met
in Libya the year before.</i>

598
00:32:38.792 --> 00:32:40.918
- Man:<i> What's your name?</i>
- Jim.

599
00:32:41.042 --> 00:32:43.542
- John. Jim and John.
- Man:<i> John. Jim.</i>

600
00:32:43.626 --> 00:32:47.042
Johnny. you say I love you.
You say.

601
00:32:47.125 --> 00:32:48.709
Diane:
<i>I don't know.</i>

602
00:32:48.792 --> 00:32:51.042
I really didn't

603
00:32:51.125 --> 00:32:53.959
really get
into it with Jim.

604
00:32:54.042 --> 00:32:56.209
I think I made it
too easy for him.

605
00:32:56.292 --> 00:32:59.375
I mean. it was something
he wanted to do.

606
00:32:59.459 --> 00:33:02.209
<i>and so we were
trying to be supportive</i>

607
00:33:02.292 --> 00:33:05.959
about his decision
to do that. you know?

608
00:33:06.042 --> 00:33:08.500
You just want to
punch him in the face.

609
00:33:08.584 --> 00:33:12.167
You know. in a loving.
brotherly way.

610
00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:15.042
But you're like.
"Come on. Jim! Come on!"

611
00:33:17.083 --> 00:33:19.667
Tom:
<i>The last conversation I ever</i>
<i>had with Jim. I said to him.</i>

612
00:33:19.751 --> 00:33:22.209
<i>"Jim. man.
why do you keep going
back into Syria?</i>

613
00:33:22.292 --> 00:33:23.918
I mean. what's it like?"

614
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:25.584
And he's like.
"It's crazy. it's crazy."

615
00:33:25.667 --> 00:33:28.000
I'm like "Well. is it more
dangerous than Libya?"

616
00:33:28.083 --> 00:33:29.584
He's like. "Yeah. it's more
dangerous than Libya!"

617
00:33:29.667 --> 00:33:31.626
I'm like.
"You got captured in Libya!"

618
00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.125
You couldn't
talk him out of it.

619
00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:15.459
( chanting )

620
00:34:15.542 --> 00:34:18.417
Jim:<i> You know.</i>
<i>the thing is. is there's</i>
<i>physical courage. right?</i>

621
00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:20.876
<i>For some reason.
I have physical courage.</i>

622
00:34:20.959 --> 00:34:22.459
<i>But really. think about it.</i>

623
00:34:22.542 --> 00:34:24.459
<i>that's nothing
compared to moral courage.</i>

624
00:34:24.542 --> 00:34:26.375
<i>I can go
and get those shots.</i>

625
00:34:26.459 --> 00:34:28.292
<i>but if I don't have
the moral courage</i>

626
00:34:28.375 --> 00:34:31.751
<i>to challenge authority.
to write about things</i>

627
00:34:31.834 --> 00:34:35.125
<i>that are going to maybe have
reprisals on my career--</i>

628
00:34:35.209 --> 00:34:37.959
<i>if I don't have moral courage.
we don't have journalism.</i>

629
00:34:40.125 --> 00:34:43.209
Jim chose one story
in particular about
this hospital.

630
00:34:43.292 --> 00:34:45.709
<i>the Dar Al Shifa
Hospital in Aleppo.</i>

631
00:34:45.792 --> 00:34:49.500
<i>It was actually
Jim's idea to spend
a week in that hospital</i>

632
00:34:49.584 --> 00:34:52.500
<i>documenting what the doctors
and the staff there</i>

633
00:34:52.584 --> 00:34:54.542
<i>were doing
on a daily basis.</i>

634
00:34:56.751 --> 00:34:58.209
Yes.

635
00:35:04.334 --> 00:35:06.584
- Whew!
- ( man shouts )

636
00:35:06.667 --> 00:35:07.959
( chanting )

637
00:35:14.709 --> 00:35:16.292
Nicole:
<i>From what he</i>
<i>and I witnessed.</i>

638
00:35:16.375 --> 00:35:18.459
they were literally
shuttling people with drips

639
00:35:18.542 --> 00:35:20.292
<i>and bandages
and everything</i>

640
00:35:20.375 --> 00:35:23.626
<i>in these tiny
little taxis and cars.</i>

641
00:35:23.709 --> 00:35:26.000
<i>And Jim was the one
who came up with the idea</i>

642
00:35:26.083 --> 00:35:29.083
<i>of raising money
to get an ambulance
for the hospital.</i>

643
00:35:29.167 --> 00:35:30.751
And he was in touch
with everybody.

644
00:35:30.834 --> 00:35:32.375
and there was
an ambulance

645
00:35:32.459 --> 00:35:33.709
that was
a secondhand ambulance

646
00:35:33.792 --> 00:35:35.542
<i>that was coming
from Austria.</i>

647
00:35:35.626 --> 00:35:37.667
<i>and Jim and I actually
made one trip in</i>

648
00:35:37.751 --> 00:35:39.876
<i>and we saw the ambulance
sitting outside the hospital.</i>

649
00:35:39.959 --> 00:35:44.292
And that moment of joy
on his face was priceless.

650
00:35:44.375 --> 00:35:46.626
<i>He was just like.
"It made it. It's awesome."</i>

651
00:35:49.918 --> 00:35:53.500
Clare:
<i>I would say the flip side</i>
<i>of his willingness</i>

652
00:35:53.584 --> 00:35:55.417
<i>to get out there
and do things</i>

653
00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:59.125
is that like.
okay. so you get
an ambulance into Syria.

654
00:35:59.209 --> 00:36:00.876
Some militia is
going to commandeer it

655
00:36:00.959 --> 00:36:03.000
<i>and use it
for their own purposes.</i>

656
00:36:03.083 --> 00:36:04.667
<i>They're going to
put an anti-aircraft
in the back of it.</i>

657
00:36:04.751 --> 00:36:06.083
like. go blow shit up.

658
00:36:06.167 --> 00:36:10.375
Jim was not focused
on that kind of issue.

659
00:36:10.459 --> 00:36:14.542
He would think about what's
the best-case scenario.

660
00:36:14.626 --> 00:36:17.500
<i>not how things
could go wrong.</i>

661
00:36:20.876 --> 00:36:22.709
Nicole:
<i>It becomes very personal</i>

662
00:36:22.792 --> 00:36:25.918
<i>because we have to live
amongst the local population.</i>

663
00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:29.918
<i>and it's such this brutal.
endless conflict</i>

664
00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.083
that you just--

665
00:36:32.167 --> 00:36:35.292
you feel so kind of alone

666
00:36:35.375 --> 00:36:37.375
in the sense
that you can't do
anything about it.

667
00:36:37.459 --> 00:36:39.709
<i>You can't do anything
for these people--</i>

668
00:36:39.792 --> 00:36:41.542
<i>for the people
you've made friends with</i>

669
00:36:41.626 --> 00:36:43.167
<i>and for the people
who took you in</i>

670
00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:45.000
<i>and shared
their food with you</i>

671
00:36:45.083 --> 00:36:47.876
<i>and wanted you to
play with their children.</i>

672
00:36:47.959 --> 00:36:50.542
We'd become the intimate
chroniclers of this conflict.

673
00:36:50.626 --> 00:36:52.417
We don't have bureaus
to go back to.

674
00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:54.834
<i>You're there.
and every moment of it</i>

675
00:36:54.918 --> 00:36:57.876
<i>you share with the locals.</i>

676
00:36:57.959 --> 00:37:01.626
And I think there was
just this enormous guilt
that rode on Jim's back

677
00:37:01.709 --> 00:37:04.751
<i>that made him
feel so compelled</i>

678
00:37:04.834 --> 00:37:08.918
<i>to do much more
than just record video
and file it.</i>

679
00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.667
<i>There were times where he was
offering up video for free</i>

680
00:37:11.751 --> 00:37:14.459
and I would chastise
him for it. I'd be like.
"What are you doing?"

681
00:37:14.542 --> 00:37:17.250
He was like. "Nah. whatever.
It's fine. It's all good.

682
00:37:17.334 --> 00:37:19.876
<i>I just want to make sure
the video gets out there."</i>

683
00:37:23.209 --> 00:37:27.083
Diane:
<i>Jim's the kind of guy</i>
<i>who never needed much.</i>

684
00:37:27.167 --> 00:37:31.375
Some of the lifestyle
of a conflict journalist
is tough.

685
00:37:31.459 --> 00:37:33.959
<i>but that didn't bother Jim.</i>

686
00:37:34.042 --> 00:37:35.959
The only possessions
I think Jim cared about

687
00:37:36.042 --> 00:37:37.709
<i>were books and CDs.</i>

688
00:37:37.792 --> 00:37:39.667
<i>I mean his camera.
ultimately.</i>

689
00:37:39.751 --> 00:37:41.792
He'd come home
without a toothbrush.

690
00:37:41.876 --> 00:37:45.167
<i>Just use whatever
toothbrush was available.</i>

691
00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:47.417
Jim could fall asleep
anywhere.

692
00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:51.209
<i>All he needed
was just a little space
on the floor.</i>

693
00:37:51.292 --> 00:37:52.500
<i>He was like a cat.</i>

694
00:37:54.209 --> 00:37:57.209
Brian:
This is a good one.

695
00:37:57.292 --> 00:37:59.334
<i>( Manu speaking )</i>

696
00:38:05.709 --> 00:38:07.667
Diane:
<i>Over time. you know.</i>

697
00:38:07.751 --> 00:38:09.334
<i>he slowly got rid
of his apartment.</i>

698
00:38:09.417 --> 00:38:10.751
<i>sold his car.</i>

699
00:38:10.834 --> 00:38:15.042
<i>He just ended up
really owning nothing.</i>

700
00:38:15.125 --> 00:38:16.709
<i>So. what would
he want for Christmas?</i>

701
00:38:16.792 --> 00:38:18.334
<i>This last time
before going to Syria.</i>

702
00:38:18.417 --> 00:38:20.292
<i>he wanted a tough
pair of pants.</i>

703
00:38:20.375 --> 00:38:21.876
<i>He really did
have less and less</i>

704
00:38:21.959 --> 00:38:24.125
and it didn't
bother him at all

705
00:38:24.209 --> 00:38:25.876
because he seemed
richer and richer.

706
00:38:38.667 --> 00:38:40.709
Nicole:
<i>There was one day</i>
<i>in August</i>

707
00:38:40.792 --> 00:38:43.792
<i>when a Syrian activist
was taking us around
this neighborhood</i>

708
00:38:43.876 --> 00:38:46.250
<i>called Bustan al-Qasr</i>

709
00:38:46.334 --> 00:38:50.584
<i>and this fighter jet
just started circling above</i>

710
00:38:50.667 --> 00:38:53.500
<i>and just swooped right down
and hit a building</i>

711
00:38:53.584 --> 00:38:55.667
<i>that was a couple hundred
feet from us.</i>

712
00:38:55.751 --> 00:38:58.250
-<i> ( gunfire )</i>
-<i> ( explosions )</i>

713
00:39:01.459 --> 00:39:03.834
<i>We started seeing
civilians coming out</i>

714
00:39:03.918 --> 00:39:07.250
<i>and just clutching
nothing. really.</i>

715
00:39:07.334 --> 00:39:08.626
<i>Just ashen faces.</i>

716
00:39:08.709 --> 00:39:10.918
<i>There was rubble everywhere.</i>

717
00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:12.209
It was chaos.

718
00:39:12.292 --> 00:39:14.083
( shouting )

719
00:39:16.334 --> 00:39:17.918
<i>And the plane
came around again.</i>

720
00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:20.042
It dropped another bomb
really close by.

721
00:39:20.125 --> 00:39:22.125
and. actually.
we were right across
the building.

722
00:39:22.209 --> 00:39:24.125
<i>and we looked up
and we could see</i>

723
00:39:24.209 --> 00:39:26.417
<i>the rubble start
coming down.</i>

724
00:39:31.209 --> 00:39:36.209
That bomb had hit
a family of seven.

725
00:39:37.292 --> 00:39:39.417
( speaking Arabic )

726
00:39:41.918 --> 00:39:43.083
Jim:
<i>Who was killed?</i>

727
00:39:44.209 --> 00:39:45.709
<i>Who?</i>

728
00:39:49.626 --> 00:39:51.584
Nicole:
<i>It was horrific in</i>
<i>the scale of it.</i>

729
00:39:51.667 --> 00:39:54.292
but also just--
I think nothing prepares you

730
00:39:54.375 --> 00:39:58.042
for seeing kids being killed
and maimed in that way.

731
00:39:58.125 --> 00:40:00.375
and I know that Jim
really loves kids.

732
00:40:00.459 --> 00:40:02.834
So. you know.
we were both just--

733
00:40:02.918 --> 00:40:06.918
we didn't say
anything until we got
to the field hospital

734
00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:08.459
where they were
bringing the bodies.

735
00:40:14.834 --> 00:40:17.918
<i>And we were both
just in this mode</i>

736
00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.792
<i>of just needing to get
the pictures out.</i>

737
00:40:20.876 --> 00:40:23.250
I think when
we were finished
that night. though.

738
00:40:23.334 --> 00:40:25.834
we kind of sat down
and lit a cigarette

739
00:40:25.918 --> 00:40:28.626
and we just started
talking about it.

740
00:40:28.709 --> 00:40:30.792
Really. there wasn't
very much to say. though.

741
00:40:30.876 --> 00:40:33.834
You know. like.
what is there to talk about

742
00:40:33.918 --> 00:40:36.250
<i>when you witness
something like that?</i>

743
00:40:36.334 --> 00:40:38.250
<i>So we just sort of
sat in silence.</i>

744
00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:42.417
Zac:
<i>Nothing prepares</i>
<i>you for that.</i>

745
00:40:42.500 --> 00:40:44.667
<i>No amount of courses.
Nothing.</i>

746
00:40:44.751 --> 00:40:48.667
<i>You just go there
and you'll either handle it
or you don't.</i>

747
00:40:48.751 --> 00:40:50.334
<i>and that's cool.</i>

748
00:40:50.417 --> 00:40:52.918
Either you run
or you stay.

749
00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.834
And there's--
not one is better
than the other.

750
00:40:55.918 --> 00:40:58.167
<i>but just don't
delude yourself.</i>

751
00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:00.709
<i>Some people aren't
meant for that.</i>

752
00:41:11.125 --> 00:41:14.876
Clare:
<i>The shine was starting</i>
<i>to come off in a way.</i>

753
00:41:14.959 --> 00:41:17.000
<i>There was a period of time</i>

754
00:41:17.083 --> 00:41:19.209
where journalists
were welcomed with open arms

755
00:41:19.292 --> 00:41:22.209
<i>because they'd seen
what had happened in Libya.</i>

756
00:41:22.292 --> 00:41:25.000
<i>And when that didn't
come about after a year.</i>

757
00:41:25.083 --> 00:41:27.751
<i>after a year and a half.
after two years.</i>

758
00:41:27.834 --> 00:41:29.667
<i>it's just like. "Okay.
what are you guys doing?"</i>

759
00:41:29.751 --> 00:41:32.334
I had a doctor
tell me at the hospital

760
00:41:32.417 --> 00:41:34.667
<i>that Jim helped raise money
for an ambulance--</i>

761
00:41:34.751 --> 00:41:38.000
<i>he was like. "Look. you guys
are in and out of here
since one year.</i>

762
00:41:38.083 --> 00:41:40.584
and it's the same exact thing
except it's worse.

763
00:41:40.667 --> 00:41:42.918
<i>I don't want to
talk to you."</i>

764
00:41:45.834 --> 00:41:47.918
<i>If the populace
on the ground</i>

765
00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:50.209
<i>whose side
you're documenting</i>

766
00:41:50.292 --> 00:41:53.584
<i>is getting
more uneasy with you</i>

767
00:41:53.667 --> 00:41:56.292
<i>or less willing to help--</i>

768
00:41:56.375 --> 00:41:57.918
you're very dependent
on the goodwill

769
00:41:58.000 --> 00:41:59.709
of the people
you're around.

770
00:42:02.209 --> 00:42:04.542
<i>( Manu speaking )</i>

771
00:42:28.584 --> 00:42:33.000
It's just that moment of.
like. how well can you
know anybody?

772
00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:36.584
<i>Even. say you know
someone very well.</i>

773
00:42:36.667 --> 00:42:38.334
<i>two years of war.
three years of war.</i>

774
00:42:38.417 --> 00:42:40.876
<i>that's going
to change anybody.</i>

775
00:42:40.959 --> 00:42:42.209
They warned journalists.

776
00:42:42.292 --> 00:42:44.125
They were like.
"Al-Qaeda is coming."

777
00:42:44.209 --> 00:42:45.876
Maybe even worse
than al-Qaeda is coming

778
00:42:45.959 --> 00:42:49.167
and nobody is going to help us
against the Assad regime

779
00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:51.792
except for these guys.

780
00:42:51.876 --> 00:42:53.626
<i>So it was all there.</i>

781
00:42:57.584 --> 00:42:59.751
Clare:
<i>One of the main things</i>
<i>I noticed</i>

782
00:42:59.834 --> 00:43:01.959
<i>the last time
when he came out.</i>

783
00:43:02.042 --> 00:43:03.250
<i>he looked really hollow</i>

784
00:43:03.334 --> 00:43:05.834
and he was quite--
quite silent.

785
00:43:05.918 --> 00:43:10.125
<i>He had that amazing
room-brightening smile</i>

786
00:43:10.209 --> 00:43:14.083
<i>even if he had seen
terrible things. as one does.</i>

787
00:43:14.167 --> 00:43:16.000
It was disheartening
to see.

788
00:43:20.709 --> 00:43:23.918
Mark:
<i>Before he left for Syria.</i>
<i>I think we made it a point.</i>

789
00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.000
we were going to bring him down
to the Faneuil Hall district

790
00:43:27.083 --> 00:43:29.792
and go to the comedy club
down there.

791
00:43:29.876 --> 00:43:31.167
<i>The comedians
were horrible.</i>

792
00:43:31.250 --> 00:43:32.751
<i>but I was in
the mood to laugh.</i>

793
00:43:32.834 --> 00:43:34.626
<i>so I'm just
laughing at anything.</i>

794
00:43:34.709 --> 00:43:36.209
And I just remember
looking at Jim

795
00:43:36.292 --> 00:43:38.500
and he was just
dead-faced.

796
00:43:38.584 --> 00:43:42.000
<i>And then me and Jim
went outside for a cigarette</i>

797
00:43:42.083 --> 00:43:44.042
<i>like we always do.</i>

798
00:43:44.125 --> 00:43:45.459
<i>He said he had to go.</i>

799
00:43:45.542 --> 00:43:47.125
<i>We had a long hug.</i>

800
00:43:47.209 --> 00:43:49.709
<i>I remember I hugged him
a really long time.</i>

801
00:43:49.792 --> 00:43:50.959
<i>Just hugged him
really tight.</i>

802
00:43:51.042 --> 00:43:52.792
<i>I said be careful.</i>

803
00:43:52.876 --> 00:43:54.584
Obviously. I didn't feel
like that was the last time

804
00:43:54.667 --> 00:43:58.000
I was going to see him.
but it was a good night.

805
00:43:58.083 --> 00:43:59.667
It was a good end
of the night. you know?

806
00:44:02.834 --> 00:44:05.459
John. Sr.:
<i>I guess if I had</i>
<i>any regrets. Brian.</i>

807
00:44:05.542 --> 00:44:09.209
I regret that
I found it difficult
to communicate with Jim.

808
00:44:09.292 --> 00:44:12.584
<i>I don't know
if it's the male thing
or whatever it is.</i>

809
00:44:12.667 --> 00:44:15.209
<i>but I just wish
I was able to share more</i>

810
00:44:15.292 --> 00:44:16.959
<i>of who I was
with Jim again</i>

811
00:44:17.042 --> 00:44:18.751
<i>and get him to share
who he was with me.</i>

812
00:44:18.834 --> 00:44:21.751
which might have been
just as difficult. you know?

813
00:44:21.834 --> 00:44:24.500
Diane:
<i>He would interview us</i>
<i>when we came home</i>

814
00:44:24.584 --> 00:44:26.500
<i>and he did a great job
interviewing us.</i>

815
00:44:26.584 --> 00:44:30.375
<i>because you felt like talking
because he was listening.</i>

816
00:44:30.459 --> 00:44:32.500
He wanted to know
how we were.

817
00:44:32.584 --> 00:44:33.792
And that was--
when he came home.

818
00:44:33.876 --> 00:44:35.459
that's what
he wanted to know.

819
00:44:35.542 --> 00:44:39.042
He wanted.
"Well. how are you?
How you doing?"

820
00:44:39.125 --> 00:44:41.959
<i>"How's Grandma?
How's Katie and Mark?"</i>

821
00:44:42.042 --> 00:44:45.083
<i>And he just
wanted to know how
everyone was doing.</i>

822
00:44:45.167 --> 00:44:49.000
<i>So in that way.
Jim was kind of solitary.</i>

823
00:44:51.125 --> 00:44:53.125
Kristie:
<i>He was home.</i>
<i>end of October.</i>

824
00:44:53.209 --> 00:44:56.542
right before he went back
to Syria that last time.

825
00:44:56.626 --> 00:44:58.500
and he was going--

826
00:44:58.584 --> 00:44:59.834
I remember
he was leaving here

827
00:44:59.918 --> 00:45:01.500
and he was going to
New York to get a helmet

828
00:45:01.584 --> 00:45:03.542
from somebody.
which was good.

829
00:45:03.626 --> 00:45:06.375
We were like.
"Getting safety equipment.
We like this."

830
00:45:06.459 --> 00:45:10.417
Yeah. I remember we left
and we dropped him off
at a train station.

831
00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:13.000
And he-- we were going
to see him in December.

832
00:45:13.083 --> 00:45:15.334
- He was supposed to
come home kind of--
- You have a good memory.

833
00:45:15.417 --> 00:45:17.042
He was supposed to
come home mid-December.

834
00:45:17.125 --> 00:45:21.125
And we were like.
"Be safe. See you soon."

835
00:45:21.209 --> 00:45:24.876
And unfortunately.
that didn't--

836
00:45:24.959 --> 00:45:27.042
Yeah.

837
00:45:32.042 --> 00:45:34.584
- Sorry.
- It's okay.

838
00:45:49.209 --> 00:45:52.417
Nicole:
<i>We'd spent the beginning</i>
<i>of November in Aleppo again</i>

839
00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:56.834
<i>with Jim. John Cantlie.
and Mustafa. our translator.</i>

840
00:45:56.918 --> 00:45:59.125
<i>who's become
a friend of ours.</i>

841
00:45:59.209 --> 00:46:02.500
I had had some issues
with my camera that week.

842
00:46:02.584 --> 00:46:06.000
<i>So. I was like. "Jim. I got
to go back to Istanbul.</i>

843
00:46:06.083 --> 00:46:08.500
<i>I'll see you guys
in a week."</i>

844
00:46:08.584 --> 00:46:12.918
The moment when
I said bye to Jim.

845
00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:17.292
<i>I had this feeling
of reluctance to leave.</i>

846
00:46:17.375 --> 00:46:20.542
<i>I think in a way
maybe it did upset
the balance</i>

847
00:46:20.626 --> 00:46:23.167
<i>that he and I
had shared for so long.</i>

848
00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:26.209
<i>There are superstitions
when you're in a war zone.</i>

849
00:46:29.918 --> 00:46:32.500
<i>There's like this one thing
that he and I shared.</i>

850
00:46:32.584 --> 00:46:34.125
which was our
lucky lighter.

851
00:46:34.209 --> 00:46:35.959
<i>It's very common
in the Middle East.</i>

852
00:46:36.042 --> 00:46:39.042
<i>It's like the evil eye
to ward off evil spirits.</i>

853
00:46:39.125 --> 00:46:41.000
<i>We'd used it
for everything.</i>

854
00:46:41.083 --> 00:46:43.042
<i>and for some reason it never
ran out of lighter fluid.</i>

855
00:46:43.125 --> 00:46:44.959
It's just this stupid idea.

856
00:46:45.042 --> 00:46:49.918
You put your hopes into one
object to make it feel safe.

857
00:46:51.334 --> 00:46:53.584
<i>I think about it
a lot afterwards.</i>

858
00:46:53.667 --> 00:46:56.375
<i>that he didn't
have it with him.</i>

859
00:46:56.459 --> 00:47:00.792
Maybe if I just gave him
the lucky lighter.

860
00:47:00.876 --> 00:47:02.751
everything might have
turned out okay.

861
00:47:02.834 --> 00:47:04.250
I don't know.

862
00:47:06.709 --> 00:47:09.375
<i>That day.
I was in Reyhanli.</i>

863
00:47:09.459 --> 00:47:11.125
<i>which is the border town.</i>

864
00:47:11.209 --> 00:47:14.250
<i>and I would have
seen him at about 5:00.</i>

865
00:47:14.334 --> 00:47:16.792
So I checked in
and I told Jim.

866
00:47:16.876 --> 00:47:18.918
I'm like.
"Hey. I'm here.

867
00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.584
so text me
when you get in."

868
00:47:22.959 --> 00:47:26.667
<i>5:00 rolls by.
and I started to worry.</i>

869
00:47:26.751 --> 00:47:28.792
<i>7:00.
8:00 rolls around.</i>

870
00:47:28.876 --> 00:47:30.500
<i>and I'm like.
"Something is really wrong."</i>

871
00:47:30.584 --> 00:47:33.375
<i>So I called Mustafa.</i>

872
00:47:33.459 --> 00:47:36.542
and the first thing
he said to me was.

873
00:47:36.626 --> 00:47:39.584
"Nicole. I'm so sorry.

874
00:47:39.667 --> 00:47:41.542
I didn't--
I couldn't do anything."

875
00:47:41.626 --> 00:47:43.709
I was like.
"What are you talking about?
What happened?"

876
00:47:43.792 --> 00:47:46.959
<i>He was like.
"We were coming.
We were in the taxi.</i>

877
00:47:47.042 --> 00:47:48.751
<i>We were coming
to Turkey to meet you.</i>

878
00:47:48.834 --> 00:47:53.209
<i>and this van with
these four guys with guns.</i>

879
00:47:53.292 --> 00:47:55.042
<i>they stopped us
on the road</i>

880
00:47:55.125 --> 00:47:56.918
<i>and they told us
to get out.</i>

881
00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.459
<i>And they were pointing
their guns at us
and screaming."</i>

882
00:48:00.542 --> 00:48:04.626
<i>The gunman made Mustafa
tie up their hands.</i>

883
00:48:04.709 --> 00:48:08.584
<i>and they put John and Jim
into the back of their van.</i>

884
00:48:10.667 --> 00:48:14.375
So. I hung up
and I just started crying.

885
00:48:14.459 --> 00:48:16.042
I was like--

886
00:48:20.167 --> 00:48:21.751
I didn't know if I was
gonna see Jim again.

887
00:48:21.834 --> 00:48:24.626
and that was the first thought
that came to my head.

888
00:48:30.542 --> 00:48:32.584
John. Sr.:
<i>It was almost surreal.</i>

889
00:48:32.667 --> 00:48:35.459
This is a bad dream.
It's not really happening.

890
00:48:35.542 --> 00:48:38.918
It's not happening.
This can't be--

891
00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:40.167
this can't happen again.

892
00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:42.500
We can't do this again.

893
00:48:45.792 --> 00:48:48.000
Yeah. it was--

894
00:48:48.083 --> 00:48:50.167
I dove in just like before.

895
00:48:50.250 --> 00:48:53.334
<i>I felt like.
"Okay. it's going to be 45
to 100 days of hell.</i>

896
00:48:53.417 --> 00:48:55.834
<i>and then
we'll have him back."</i>

897
00:48:55.918 --> 00:48:58.876
<i>In a matter of a week.
you could tell it was
very different.</i>

898
00:48:58.959 --> 00:49:01.834
A lot of misinformation.
as opposed to last time
in Libya--

899
00:49:01.918 --> 00:49:05.834
after a week went by
we knew where he was.
who to deal with.

900
00:49:05.918 --> 00:49:08.375
so we just concentrated on
routes to get to one person.

901
00:49:08.459 --> 00:49:11.792
<i>Here was a mystery
right out of a crime show
or something</i>

902
00:49:11.876 --> 00:49:15.500
<i>where you're trying
to piece together bits
of information.</i>

903
00:49:15.584 --> 00:49:17.375
Nicole:
<i>For the next three weeks.</i>

904
00:49:17.459 --> 00:49:20.459
there was just dead ends
and false information

905
00:49:20.542 --> 00:49:23.250
and rumors and people
being scared of talking

906
00:49:23.334 --> 00:49:27.542
<i>because they had a suspicion
of who maybe was responsible</i>

907
00:49:27.626 --> 00:49:29.459
<i>and they didn't want to
get entangled in it.</i>

908
00:49:29.542 --> 00:49:33.709
Diane:
<i>Phil Balboni offered</i>
<i>to stand up</i>

909
00:49:33.792 --> 00:49:35.918
<i>a security team
to try to find Jim.</i>

910
00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:38.626
So. all these people
were trying so hard

911
00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:41.375
to get in place.
but it was a very.

912
00:49:41.459 --> 00:49:43.375
very chaotic.
confusing time.

913
00:49:46.250 --> 00:49:50.083
John. Jr.:
<i>You're on eggshells.</i>
<i>You're just waiting to hear.</i>

914
00:49:50.167 --> 00:49:52.876
It's exhausting.
and I know Jim felt
guilty for that.

915
00:49:52.959 --> 00:49:56.626
and I'm not trying to
make him feel more guilty.

916
00:49:56.709 --> 00:49:58.542
but it's just a toll

917
00:49:58.626 --> 00:50:03.000
that's taken
by the families.

918
00:50:03.083 --> 00:50:05.000
Brian:
<i>What were the hurdles</i>
<i>getting the White House</i>

919
00:50:05.083 --> 00:50:06.584
<i>and FBI involved?</i>

920
00:50:06.667 --> 00:50:09.000
Michael:
<i>It was very tough</i>
<i>to get action.</i>

921
00:50:09.083 --> 00:50:11.250
<i>And I understand that
the world is a big place.</i>

922
00:50:11.334 --> 00:50:14.459
<i>and so I actually
felt guilty sometimes</i>

923
00:50:14.542 --> 00:50:17.375
<i>trying not to ask
too much of them.</i>

924
00:50:17.459 --> 00:50:18.584
Jim made this decision.

925
00:50:18.667 --> 00:50:21.083
but just give it
your best attention

926
00:50:21.167 --> 00:50:22.918
and we'll trust you.

927
00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:26.209
That's kind of
where it started.
the relationship.

928
00:50:26.292 --> 00:50:29.209
Then I met the first agent
that came over

929
00:50:29.292 --> 00:50:33.042
and it was just a kid
out of school.

930
00:50:33.125 --> 00:50:36.876
and his first question
to me was had I asked
the regime for assistance.

931
00:50:36.959 --> 00:50:38.709
<i>Are you
fucking kidding me?</i>

932
00:50:38.792 --> 00:50:41.542
Have I called the regime
and asked them for assistance?

933
00:50:41.626 --> 00:50:43.584
No. I hadn't thought of that.
Thank you.

934
00:50:43.667 --> 00:50:46.292
Thank you very much
for that tip.

935
00:50:46.375 --> 00:50:49.459
They told us--
they advised us
to be quiet.

936
00:50:49.542 --> 00:50:52.334
because hopefully
they could find him

937
00:50:52.417 --> 00:50:53.792
and get him out and such.

938
00:50:53.876 --> 00:50:55.584
So we didn't say anything.

939
00:50:55.667 --> 00:50:58.292
<i>We went through
Christmas and all that</i>

940
00:50:58.375 --> 00:51:01.834
<i>not telling anyone
but our closest family
that Jim was missing.</i>

941
00:51:04.209 --> 00:51:06.584
Tom:
<i>In some ways</i>
<i>it was better</i>

942
00:51:06.667 --> 00:51:09.584
because I didn't
have to explain it.

943
00:51:09.667 --> 00:51:12.250
Because at times
it could feel accusatory.

944
00:51:12.334 --> 00:51:14.500
like. "Well. he was
in Syria."

945
00:51:14.584 --> 00:51:17.792
That's not fair.

946
00:51:17.876 --> 00:51:19.584
You don't do that
with police or firemen

947
00:51:19.667 --> 00:51:21.167
or something like that
who do dangerous jobs.

948
00:51:21.250 --> 00:51:22.500
You don't say.
"Well. you were in a fire.

949
00:51:22.584 --> 00:51:24.459
What did you think
would happen?"

950
00:51:24.542 --> 00:51:27.667
My friend doesn't need
to explain why he's
a journalist.

951
00:51:29.834 --> 00:51:33.000
Diane:
<i>Come the new year.</i>
<i>I couldn't stand it.</i>

952
00:51:33.083 --> 00:51:34.417
<i>I was frantic.</i>

953
00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:37.584
So we chose to go public.

954
00:51:37.667 --> 00:51:40.667
John. Sr.:
<i>I appeal to the people</i>
<i>who have Jim</i>

955
00:51:40.751 --> 00:51:44.959
to give us some information
in terms of his welfare.
his health.

956
00:51:45.042 --> 00:51:46.542
Diane:
<i>It breaks my heart</i>

957
00:51:46.626 --> 00:51:50.292
that the persons
who have captured him

958
00:51:50.375 --> 00:51:53.709
don't understand
his goodness.

959
00:51:53.792 --> 00:51:57.751
My personal feeling is that
silence helps two people.

960
00:51:57.834 --> 00:51:59.250
One is the government--

961
00:51:59.334 --> 00:52:02.125
it doesn't push them
to do more sooner--

962
00:52:02.209 --> 00:52:03.542
and the captors.

963
00:52:03.626 --> 00:52:06.542
<i>It allows them to do
whatever they want.</i>

964
00:52:06.626 --> 00:52:08.250
It's difficult now

965
00:52:08.334 --> 00:52:11.209
with all the talk
about the Islamic State.

966
00:52:11.292 --> 00:52:15.334
<i>They've become
so famous or infamous.</i>

967
00:52:15.417 --> 00:52:18.334
<i>But the Islamic State.
ISIS. ISIL.</i>

968
00:52:18.417 --> 00:52:22.417
<i>was on nobody's
radar at the time.</i>

969
00:52:22.500 --> 00:52:24.584
With a very high
degree of confidence.

970
00:52:24.667 --> 00:52:27.918
<i>we now believe
that Jim was abducted</i>

971
00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:29.918
<i>by a pro-government
militia group</i>

972
00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:31.500
and was subsequently
turned over

973
00:52:31.584 --> 00:52:33.167
to Syrian
government forces.

974
00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:34.417
This is the first time

975
00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:36.500
we've really heard
anything like this.

976
00:52:36.584 --> 00:52:40.667
so we are very hopeful.
like John says.

977
00:52:40.751 --> 00:52:43.083
Well. it turned out
we were dead wrong about that.

978
00:52:45.375 --> 00:52:48.083
Michael:
<i>All the information up front</i>
<i>was just a bunch of BS.</i>

979
00:52:48.167 --> 00:52:50.083
<i>No one knew what the heck
they were talking about</i>

980
00:52:50.167 --> 00:52:51.918
<i>because we had no access.</i>

981
00:52:52.000 --> 00:52:54.918
That whole year.
I don't care what anyone says.

982
00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:56.751
all the efforts.
all the leads.
all this and that...

983
00:52:58.375 --> 00:53:01.542
we were in the wrong area
of the country. you know?

984
00:53:01.626 --> 00:53:03.542
<i>Syria is about the size
of New England. maybe?</i>

985
00:53:03.626 --> 00:53:05.584
A little smaller.
So here you go. Brian.

986
00:53:05.667 --> 00:53:09.167
Go into New England.
Find Jim.

987
00:53:09.250 --> 00:53:11.667
I think the moment
that I learned that he had
been kidnapped.

988
00:53:11.751 --> 00:53:15.751
I was just like.
this is going to be
a really long process.

989
00:53:15.834 --> 00:53:17.375
But I'm going
to do anything

990
00:53:17.459 --> 00:53:20.125
that I can in my power
to get him home.

991
00:53:20.209 --> 00:53:24.709
Because I can't stand
the thought of him

992
00:53:24.792 --> 00:53:28.042
being in a cell somewhere
cold and hungry.

993
00:53:28.125 --> 00:53:29.751
<i>And I can't sit here</i>

994
00:53:29.834 --> 00:53:31.626
<i>and not try
to look for him.</i>

995
00:53:49.375 --> 00:53:51.083
Daniel:
<i>My name is Daniel.</i>

996
00:53:51.167 --> 00:53:53.334
<i>and I'm a Danish
photojournalist.</i>

997
00:53:54.542 --> 00:53:57.584
<i>I started as a gymnast.</i>

998
00:53:57.667 --> 00:54:00.167
<i>and while I spent
all my time in gyms</i>

999
00:54:00.250 --> 00:54:02.751
<i>all around the world
doing gymnastics.</i>

1000
00:54:02.834 --> 00:54:05.417
I got bored when I didn't do
anything else

1001
00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:07.417
and I started to
take pictures.

1002
00:54:11.125 --> 00:54:16.167
<i>I only planned to be inside
Syria for two days.</i>

1003
00:54:16.250 --> 00:54:19.250
I had one day of work

1004
00:54:19.334 --> 00:54:23.083
when I walked around
this small quiet town.

1005
00:54:24.250 --> 00:54:27.083
<i>It was spring.</i>

1006
00:54:27.167 --> 00:54:29.667
<i>so the weather
started to be very better.</i>

1007
00:54:29.751 --> 00:54:33.667
<i>and people seemed
happy and relaxed</i>

1008
00:54:33.751 --> 00:54:37.292
<i>and we were told
to go and speak</i>

1009
00:54:37.375 --> 00:54:40.250
<i>to some guys in the area.</i>

1010
00:54:42.709 --> 00:54:44.459
<i>Very calmly.
we're sitting on sofas.</i>

1011
00:54:44.542 --> 00:54:47.250
<i>They were offering tea.</i>

1012
00:54:47.334 --> 00:54:49.584
<i>Everything
was calm and quiet.</i>

1013
00:54:49.667 --> 00:54:52.709
even though I knew that
something was strange.

1014
00:54:52.792 --> 00:54:54.250
something was wrong.

1015
00:54:54.334 --> 00:54:57.584
And then they
asked me to stand up

1016
00:54:57.667 --> 00:54:59.584
<i>and they took off
my glasses.</i>

1017
00:54:59.667 --> 00:55:03.918
<i>and they just-- "Don't worry.
this is just a procedure."</i>

1018
00:55:06.626 --> 00:55:08.042
<i>That was how
everything began.</i>

1019
00:55:08.125 --> 00:55:10.167
So. you know.
a quiet Sunday

1020
00:55:10.250 --> 00:55:13.959
beautiful spring morning
became a nightmare for me.

1021
00:55:22.167 --> 00:55:24.042
<i>The longer
you are a hostage.</i>

1022
00:55:24.125 --> 00:55:26.751
<i>the easier
it becomes in some way.</i>

1023
00:55:26.834 --> 00:55:28.167
the better you get at it.

1024
00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:30.083
<i>And I had. like.
I think. one.</i>

1025
00:55:30.167 --> 00:55:32.250
<i>one and half months
by myself.</i>

1026
00:55:32.334 --> 00:55:35.209
<i>After. I believe.
two and a half months.</i>

1027
00:55:35.292 --> 00:55:38.918
<i>I was put together
with two other Westerners.</i>

1028
00:55:39.000 --> 00:55:42.167
<i>And then we were
put together four.</i>

1029
00:55:42.250 --> 00:55:43.667
<i>I think we were
five together.</i>

1030
00:55:43.751 --> 00:55:45.083
<i>and then seven together.</i>

1031
00:55:45.167 --> 00:55:47.375
<i>And then came
another one-- eight.</i>

1032
00:55:47.459 --> 00:55:49.459
<i>Then it just started
to evolve.</i>

1033
00:56:00.792 --> 00:56:02.375
<i>We were in that cell--</i>

1034
00:56:02.459 --> 00:56:05.167
<i>British. Americans.</i>

1035
00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:08.375
<i>French. Italian. German.</i>

1036
00:56:08.459 --> 00:56:11.209
<i>Danish. Russian. Spanish.</i>

1037
00:56:11.292 --> 00:56:13.334
<i>We were 19 at one stage.</i>

1038
00:57:54.125 --> 00:57:58.876
One day we had to sit
faces to the wall.

1039
00:57:58.959 --> 00:58:01.209
but I could see
under my arms.

1040
00:58:01.292 --> 00:58:04.375
<i>I could see some
mattresses were moved in.</i>

1041
00:58:04.459 --> 00:58:08.167
<i>and there came some guys
in traditional Syrian clothes.</i>

1042
00:58:08.250 --> 00:58:12.209
And then they
closed the door again.
the big metal door.

1043
00:58:12.292 --> 00:58:15.834
<i>and I looked up.
and there was James
and John Cantlie.</i>

1044
00:58:17.626 --> 00:58:19.876
Everybody was like.
"Yay! Welcome! Welcome!
Welcome!"

1045
00:58:19.959 --> 00:58:22.334
It's two new friends.

1046
00:58:22.417 --> 00:58:24.459
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1047
00:58:32.209 --> 00:58:35.125
I created this
picture in my head

1048
00:58:35.209 --> 00:58:37.918
of this big war
journalist.

1049
00:58:38.000 --> 00:58:41.125
and so I could only get
disappointed in a way.

1050
00:58:41.209 --> 00:58:43.834
But I remember
him being like...

1051
00:58:45.876 --> 00:58:47.375
"What's happening?"

1052
00:58:47.459 --> 00:58:50.083
So that was basically
the first time I met him.

1053
00:58:52.375 --> 00:58:53.918
<i>But it was real different</i>

1054
00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:56.375
<i>to be put together
with James and John.</i>

1055
00:58:56.459 --> 00:58:59.542
because they've been together
in prison for almost a year.

1056
00:58:59.626 --> 00:59:00.959
When I first saw them.

1057
00:59:01.042 --> 00:59:04.417
<i>they were the most
experienced of us.</i>

1058
00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:06.751
<i>and I started
from the beginning.</i>

1059
00:59:06.834 --> 00:59:11.000
I think the whole group
started to lean a little
bit against them.

1060
00:59:13.167 --> 00:59:15.209
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1061
00:59:58.417 --> 01:00:01.834
Daniel:
<i>James was very silent</i>
<i>most of the time.</i>

1062
01:00:01.918 --> 01:00:04.584
He was very good
at listening.

1063
01:00:04.667 --> 01:00:07.042
<i>He managed to make
the room bigger in a way</i>

1064
01:00:07.125 --> 01:00:09.292
<i>by being small himself.</i>

1065
01:00:09.375 --> 01:00:12.500
<i>and that is a very.
very difficult thing to be.</i>

1066
01:00:12.584 --> 01:00:15.751
And you really
want to scream in the head
of everybody like.

1067
01:00:15.834 --> 01:00:17.876
"Fuck off!"

1068
01:00:24.918 --> 01:00:27.334
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1069
01:01:02.250 --> 01:01:04.042
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1070
01:01:33.959 --> 01:01:36.083
Daniel:
<i>I remember one time.</i>
<i>James was asked</i>

1071
01:01:36.167 --> 01:01:41.083
to stand up the whole night
in the middle of the room.

1072
01:01:42.209 --> 01:01:44.334
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1073
01:02:04.542 --> 01:02:06.459
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1074
01:02:52.167 --> 01:02:55.292
Daniel:
<i>Late at night.</i>
<i>there was no light at all.</i>

1075
01:02:55.375 --> 01:02:58.834
<i>so we were just sitting there
in complete darkness.</i>

1076
01:02:58.918 --> 01:03:03.542
<i>and that time really.
really. really went slowly.</i>

1077
01:03:03.626 --> 01:03:05.334
<i>What we did.
James and I.</i>

1078
01:03:05.417 --> 01:03:07.083
<i>we started
to develop a way</i>

1079
01:03:07.167 --> 01:03:11.500
<i>of passing through
these hours of darkness</i>

1080
01:03:11.584 --> 01:03:15.918
<i>by giving each other.
like. massages.</i>

1081
01:03:16.042 --> 01:03:19.626
And it sounds maybe
a little bit. you know.

1082
01:03:19.709 --> 01:03:23.042
strange or gay
or whatever.

1083
01:03:23.125 --> 01:03:27.375
but there was something--

1084
01:03:27.459 --> 01:03:30.417
there was something
nice about it.

1085
01:03:30.500 --> 01:03:32.834
<i>And James asked me.
"Can you teach me</i>

1086
01:03:32.918 --> 01:03:34.792
how to give
a real nice massage.

1087
01:03:34.876 --> 01:03:37.667
so when I get out
and I meet a woman. I can--

1088
01:03:37.751 --> 01:03:40.334
I can really impress her?"

1089
01:03:40.417 --> 01:03:45.000
<i>So we started having
these kind of lessons.</i>

1090
01:03:45.083 --> 01:03:48.292
<i>Our bodies had witnessed
a lot of trauma</i>

1091
01:03:48.375 --> 01:03:51.584
<i>and the fact that somebody
is actually touching you</i>

1092
01:03:51.667 --> 01:03:54.667
<i>and it's a nice feeling.
for me.</i>

1093
01:03:54.751 --> 01:03:58.542
<i>it was a nice way
to feel a little bit
human again.</i>

1094
01:03:58.626 --> 01:04:01.959
And James. he never learned
how to give a proper massage.

1095
01:04:02.042 --> 01:04:03.250
It was awful every time.

1096
01:04:03.334 --> 01:04:05.876
So. he really--
he really managed

1097
01:04:05.959 --> 01:04:08.626
to get a good deal
out of that one.

1098
01:04:16.459 --> 01:04:19.167
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1099
01:04:40.083 --> 01:04:43.292
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1100
01:05:08.167 --> 01:05:09.626
Daniel:
<i>I remember one time.</i>

1101
01:05:09.709 --> 01:05:12.667
<i>we were given
a lot of dates to eat.</i>

1102
01:05:12.751 --> 01:05:15.042
<i>And at some point
we were moved.</i>

1103
01:05:15.125 --> 01:05:17.959
and you just don't
leave food behind

1104
01:05:18.042 --> 01:05:19.167
or destroy it or whatever.

1105
01:05:19.250 --> 01:05:20.667
but sometimes
you have to do it

1106
01:05:20.751 --> 01:05:22.876
because there's
nowhere to put it.

1107
01:05:24.083 --> 01:05:25.709
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1108
01:05:42.834 --> 01:05:44.918
James. he just
took out his pants

1109
01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:46.959
and he took out
two kilos of dates.

1110
01:05:47.042 --> 01:05:49.209
<i>"Don't worry. guys."</i>

1111
01:05:51.918 --> 01:05:54.292
<i>He could have taken
all the food by himself</i>

1112
01:05:54.375 --> 01:05:55.959
<i>later that night
or whatever.</i>

1113
01:05:56.042 --> 01:05:57.584
<i>but he always
took the things</i>

1114
01:05:57.667 --> 01:05:59.083
so he could share
it around

1115
01:05:59.167 --> 01:06:00.667
or give it to the people
who didn't have it.

1116
01:06:19.334 --> 01:06:21.042
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1117
01:07:16.751 --> 01:07:19.375
Daniel:
<i>In the beginning of James</i>
<i>and John's captivity.</i>

1118
01:07:19.459 --> 01:07:22.167
<i>they were really starved.</i>

1119
01:07:22.250 --> 01:07:24.083
They didn't like
to talk about it.

1120
01:07:24.167 --> 01:07:26.125
They didn't find it
very interesting
to talk about.

1121
01:07:26.209 --> 01:07:29.292
But one thing
I know was that...

1122
01:07:32.167 --> 01:07:34.792
they really. really.
really had a difficult time.

1123
01:07:36.334 --> 01:07:39.918
<i>But they managed
to get back on track</i>

1124
01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:43.459
<i>to gain strength again.</i>

1125
01:07:43.542 --> 01:07:45.709
<i>It was very
interesting to see</i>

1126
01:07:45.792 --> 01:07:48.167
<i>what happened
between James and John.</i>

1127
01:07:48.250 --> 01:07:50.709
because they've been
together for almost a year

1128
01:07:50.792 --> 01:07:52.250
when I first saw them.

1129
01:07:52.334 --> 01:07:54.417
That meant
they had spoken about

1130
01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:57.542
every single thing
there is to talk about.

1131
01:07:57.626 --> 01:07:59.167
So I was basically the one

1132
01:07:59.250 --> 01:08:03.459
starting to listen
to all James' stories again.

1133
01:08:07.459 --> 01:08:09.918
<i>There was a period
of time in the prison</i>

1134
01:08:10.000 --> 01:08:12.167
<i>where we were
not interrupted</i>

1135
01:08:12.250 --> 01:08:14.167
<i>by the guards very often.</i>

1136
01:08:14.250 --> 01:08:15.918
<i>That meant that
we could get a routine.</i>

1137
01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:18.876
<i>So we worked out.
we did trivia.</i>

1138
01:08:18.959 --> 01:08:21.834
<i>we had lectures.
and stuff like this.</i>

1139
01:08:21.918 --> 01:08:24.667
We managed after.
I don't know. three weeks.

1140
01:08:24.751 --> 01:08:27.709
one month or so.
to make this Risk game.

1141
01:08:28.751 --> 01:08:30.709
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1142
01:08:49.417 --> 01:08:51.209
We had a small bucket

1143
01:08:51.292 --> 01:08:52.792
that we received
some yogurt in.

1144
01:08:52.876 --> 01:08:56.334
<i>and we cut out
a piece of cardboard</i>

1145
01:08:56.417 --> 01:08:57.918
<i>and we made three lines.</i>

1146
01:08:58.000 --> 01:08:59.918
<i>and we put it in
the bottom of the bucket</i>

1147
01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:02.918
and then you should
hold up a date seed

1148
01:09:03.000 --> 01:09:04.751
and let it go.

1149
01:09:04.834 --> 01:09:06.209
<i>It would fall down.</i>

1150
01:09:06.292 --> 01:09:07.792
<i>and whatever
number it landed on.</i>

1151
01:09:07.876 --> 01:09:09.125
<i>it would be that number.</i>

1152
01:09:09.209 --> 01:09:11.459
So that was our dice
for the game.

1153
01:09:12.667 --> 01:09:14.792
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1154
01:09:23.459 --> 01:09:27.125
Daniel:
<i>Take 10 journalists. war.</i>

1155
01:09:27.209 --> 01:09:29.584
<i>put them into one room
and make them play</i>

1156
01:09:29.667 --> 01:09:34.334
<i>the game about taking
control over the world.</i>

1157
01:09:34.417 --> 01:09:37.000
It's basically like putting
gasoline to a bonfire.

1158
01:10:11.417 --> 01:10:15.918
Daniel:
<i>We started to have</i>
<i>our own small world.</i>

1159
01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:19.834
<i>That made
everything much easier
to survive in a way.</i>

1160
01:10:19.918 --> 01:10:21.918
<i>It was much easier
to understand.</i>

1161
01:10:24.083 --> 01:10:26.209
<i>We didn't have to think
about economy.</i>

1162
01:10:26.292 --> 01:10:29.626
<i>We didn't have to think
about bank loans</i>

1163
01:10:29.709 --> 01:10:34.459
<i>or the prices of gas
at the moment.</i>

1164
01:10:34.542 --> 01:10:37.209
<i>You adapt
into the situation.</i>

1165
01:10:37.292 --> 01:10:40.000
<i>and then suddenly
this whole thing</i>

1166
01:10:40.083 --> 01:10:42.500
<i>becomes a part
of your life.</i>

1167
01:10:42.584 --> 01:10:44.083
<i>This is your life.</i>

1168
01:10:44.167 --> 01:10:46.459
<i>When you look back on it.
that's what I remember.</i>

1169
01:10:46.542 --> 01:10:48.959
was our small society

1170
01:10:49.042 --> 01:10:51.626
where we really start
to know each other.

1171
01:10:51.709 --> 01:10:53.751
You know
who made this fart.

1172
01:10:53.834 --> 01:10:56.584
You could smell--
"This is the fart of you."

1173
01:10:57.751 --> 01:10:59.584
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1174
01:11:18.584 --> 01:11:22.167
Daniel:
<i>I remember James'</i>
<i>40-year-old birthday.</i>

1175
01:11:22.250 --> 01:11:25.083
<i>It was late at night.
It was completely dark.</i>

1176
01:11:25.167 --> 01:11:28.918
James just--
"Oh. by the way.

1177
01:11:29.000 --> 01:11:31.292
I turn 40 today."

1178
01:11:31.375 --> 01:11:32.792
I was just like. "What?"

1179
01:11:32.876 --> 01:11:34.626
<i>So we sang
a song for him</i>

1180
01:11:34.709 --> 01:11:37.042
<i>and I remember
that we said</i>

1181
01:11:37.125 --> 01:11:40.209
<i>we hoped it would
be a much better
birthday next year.</i>

1182
01:12:16.250 --> 01:12:19.417
( overlapping chatter )

1183
01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:22.250
Right here.
One. two. and three.

1184
01:12:22.334 --> 01:12:24.083
Come on!
Smile! Please!

1185
01:12:24.167 --> 01:12:25.709
<i>Nine! Good!</i>

1186
01:12:25.792 --> 01:12:27.542
Jim:
<i>We've been through</i>
<i>a lot together.</i>

1187
01:12:27.626 --> 01:12:30.709
<i>Michael has cosigned
loans for me.</i>

1188
01:12:30.792 --> 01:12:31.959
<i>( laughter )</i>

1189
01:12:32.042 --> 01:12:34.834
He has lent me
his professional clothes.

1190
01:12:34.918 --> 01:12:37.751
his car.
his dental plan.

1191
01:12:39.584 --> 01:12:42.292
I think sometimes
we struggle to understand
each other

1192
01:12:42.375 --> 01:12:43.876
and where exactly
we're coming from

1193
01:12:43.959 --> 01:12:46.500
and why we do
the things we do.

1194
01:12:46.584 --> 01:12:50.375
Michael has entirely
too much common sense

1195
01:12:50.459 --> 01:12:54.250
and sometimes I have entirely
too little common sense.

1196
01:12:54.334 --> 01:12:58.626
We've somehow grown closer
despite the differences.

1197
01:12:58.709 --> 01:13:02.500
and it's-- I guess
it's about being brothers.

1198
01:13:04.876 --> 01:13:08.709
Michael:
<i>I'd say the first 100.</i>
<i>150 days I was all in.</i>

1199
01:13:08.792 --> 01:13:13.500
<i>but I definitely retracted
pretty strongly after that.</i>

1200
01:13:13.584 --> 01:13:17.792
I have a lot of regrets about
not continuing full steam.

1201
01:13:17.876 --> 01:13:20.250
and I don't know
if it was because

1202
01:13:20.334 --> 01:13:22.584
I was trying
to protect myself

1203
01:13:22.667 --> 01:13:25.125
<i>or I was just trying
to protect my family</i>

1204
01:13:25.209 --> 01:13:28.876
<i>and give the kids
the attention I have.</i>

1205
01:13:28.959 --> 01:13:31.792
<i>I could argue that that's
what Jim would prefer.</i>

1206
01:13:31.876 --> 01:13:36.083
and I don't know--
it was all kinds of
excuses or reasons.

1207
01:13:36.167 --> 01:13:39.500
but it's something
I do feel terrible about.

1208
01:13:39.584 --> 01:13:41.751
<i>But then I got pulled
right back into it</i>

1209
01:13:41.834 --> 01:13:43.542
<i>very strongly
and very immediately</i>

1210
01:13:43.626 --> 01:13:46.751
when the first e-mail
came to me.

1211
01:13:46.834 --> 01:13:49.042
"Hello. we have James

1212
01:13:49.125 --> 01:13:51.209
and want to
negotiate for him.

1213
01:13:51.292 --> 01:13:52.834
He is safe.

1214
01:13:52.918 --> 01:13:56.083
He is our friend
and we do not want
to hurt him.

1215
01:13:56.167 --> 01:13:58.167
If you want cooperation.
we have rules.

1216
01:13:58.250 --> 01:14:01.250
<i>You cannot go
to the media ever
about this.</i>

1217
01:14:01.334 --> 01:14:03.167
<i>If you do.
we will not negotiate.</i>

1218
01:14:03.250 --> 01:14:05.000
<i>We want money fast."</i>

1219
01:14:05.083 --> 01:14:07.542
We shared everything
we had with everybody.

1220
01:14:07.626 --> 01:14:10.292
FBI knew.
security team knew--
everyone knew.

1221
01:14:10.375 --> 01:14:12.375
They said.
"Just keep them talking."
and all that.

1222
01:14:12.459 --> 01:14:14.417
"They're just beginning
their negotiations.

1223
01:14:14.500 --> 01:14:16.667
We've got time.
Just keep at it."

1224
01:14:16.751 --> 01:14:19.292
Michael:
<i>So. I. after coordinating</i>
<i>with some officials.</i>

1225
01:14:19.375 --> 01:14:21.959
<i>replied to them.
"We've been concerned
about Jim</i>

1226
01:14:22.042 --> 01:14:23.751
<i>and want to know
that he's okay.</i>

1227
01:14:23.834 --> 01:14:26.542
Please provide us with proof
that you have Jim.

1228
01:14:26.626 --> 01:14:29.042
<i>and we will be happy
to work things out with you."</i>

1229
01:14:29.125 --> 01:14:32.042
Diane:
<i>We still didn't know</i>
<i>who was holding him.</i>

1230
01:14:32.125 --> 01:14:35.292
It was obvious
that they were people
against the Assad regime.

1231
01:14:35.375 --> 01:14:37.125
but they didn't
identify themselves

1232
01:14:37.209 --> 01:14:38.584
any more
than that at all.

1233
01:14:38.667 --> 01:14:40.709
They were very shrewd.

1234
01:14:40.792 --> 01:14:43.209
And their e-mails.
unfortunately.

1235
01:14:43.292 --> 01:14:45.042
were totally
undetectable.

1236
01:14:45.125 --> 01:14:47.876
About a week goes by
and they responded.

1237
01:14:47.959 --> 01:14:50.834
<i>"James Wright Foley
is being detained by us.</i>

1238
01:14:50.918 --> 01:14:52.792
<i>At this stage. no video
or picture evidence</i>

1239
01:14:52.876 --> 01:14:54.667
<i>of his well-being
will be provided</i>

1240
01:14:54.751 --> 01:14:57.751
until we see tangible progress
in your efforts to negotiate.

1241
01:14:57.834 --> 01:15:00.125
<i>However. you will be allowed
to ask three questions</i>

1242
01:15:00.209 --> 01:15:01.792
<i>of a personal nature</i>

1243
01:15:01.876 --> 01:15:04.250
<i>that nobody except James
will know the answers to.</i>

1244
01:15:04.334 --> 01:15:07.125
And our primary demand is that
you use your influence

1245
01:15:07.209 --> 01:15:08.500
to pressure
your government

1246
01:15:08.584 --> 01:15:10.459
<i>to release
our Muslim prisoners</i>

1247
01:15:10.542 --> 01:15:13.083
<i>who they have imprisoned.
whether innocent</i>

1248
01:15:13.167 --> 01:15:16.667
<i>or 'guilty' according
to 'your laws.'</i>

1249
01:15:16.751 --> 01:15:19.167
<i>If this fails
to bear any fruits.</i>

1250
01:15:19.250 --> 01:15:20.626
<i>then our secondary demand</i>

1251
01:15:20.709 --> 01:15:24.209
<i>is the sum
of a hundred million euros."</i>

1252
01:15:24.292 --> 01:15:27.876
FBI seemed to have
their hands tied

1253
01:15:27.959 --> 01:15:29.751
because all
they were able to do

1254
01:15:29.834 --> 01:15:33.209
was okay
our family e-mails.

1255
01:15:33.292 --> 01:15:37.709
and they really were unable
to help us much with strategy.

1256
01:15:37.792 --> 01:15:40.751
<i>They just told us to be
yourselves. be family.</i>

1257
01:15:40.834 --> 01:15:42.292
<i>Tell them the truth</i>

1258
01:15:42.375 --> 01:15:44.876
<i>is we can't release
any prisoners.</i>

1259
01:15:44.959 --> 01:15:47.751
We certainly don't have
a hundred million euro.

1260
01:15:47.834 --> 01:15:49.792
<i>Michael came up
with some questions</i>

1261
01:15:49.876 --> 01:15:51.667
<i>I didn't know
the answers to.</i>

1262
01:15:51.751 --> 01:15:56.334
<i>but we sent those back
to the captors.</i>

1263
01:15:56.417 --> 01:15:59.167
Michael:
<i>Then there was some</i>
<i>brief comment.</i>

1264
01:15:59.250 --> 01:16:01.334
It says.
"James was detained

1265
01:16:01.417 --> 01:16:04.167
whilst operating
as a 'journalist.'

1266
01:16:04.250 --> 01:16:07.959
<i>After his capture
and following interrogations.</i>

1267
01:16:08.042 --> 01:16:09.751
<i>we came to learn
that he had been embedded</i>

1268
01:16:09.834 --> 01:16:11.751
<i>with US troops
serving in Afghanistan</i>

1269
01:16:11.834 --> 01:16:14.083
<i>and that his brother
Michael Patrick Foley</i>

1270
01:16:14.167 --> 01:16:16.751
<i>is a serving officer
in the US Air Force."</i>

1271
01:16:16.834 --> 01:16:18.417
<i>But I was never
in the Air Force.</i>

1272
01:16:18.500 --> 01:16:20.959
<i>That was John.</i>

1273
01:16:24.000 --> 01:16:27.751
My brother Jim
said that Mike

1274
01:16:27.834 --> 01:16:30.959
<i>was the Air Force
officer.</i>

1275
01:16:31.042 --> 01:16:33.167
<i>It may have been a typo.
it may not have been.</i>

1276
01:16:33.250 --> 01:16:37.459
But I believe
it was my big brother

1277
01:16:37.542 --> 01:16:40.209
trying to protect me.

1278
01:16:40.292 --> 01:16:44.709
You love
your brothers. but--

1279
01:16:49.417 --> 01:16:51.125
but for him to put--

1280
01:16:51.209 --> 01:16:53.209
be willing to put
his life on the line

1281
01:16:53.292 --> 01:16:55.250
or his body
on the line...

1282
01:17:00.500 --> 01:17:03.042
for my protection
is significant.

1283
01:17:16.918 --> 01:17:20.334
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1284
01:17:29.334 --> 01:17:32.500
And. I mean. it's...

1285
01:17:40.834 --> 01:17:43.918
Daniel:
<i>I think they came in with</i>
<i>the proof of life for John</i>

1286
01:17:44.000 --> 01:17:46.459
<i>and then everybody else
got their proof of lives.</i>

1287
01:17:46.542 --> 01:17:47.876
Everybody came in
like. "Yes!"

1288
01:17:47.959 --> 01:17:49.626
And we're happy and--

1289
01:17:49.709 --> 01:17:53.459
<i>but James didn't get
his proof of life.</i>

1290
01:17:53.542 --> 01:17:56.209
<i>Until one day
that they came in</i>

1291
01:17:56.292 --> 01:17:58.876
<i>and they asked
James to follow.</i>

1292
01:18:00.584 --> 01:18:02.083
<i>And when he came back.
you know.</i>

1293
01:18:02.167 --> 01:18:04.459
<i>he came with his arms
over his head.</i>

1294
01:18:04.542 --> 01:18:07.584
he said that this was
the best day of his life.

1295
01:18:07.667 --> 01:18:11.250
<i>And then him and John.
they hugged each other</i>

1296
01:18:11.334 --> 01:18:12.751
<i>and they were
dancing around</i>

1297
01:18:12.834 --> 01:18:16.042
<i>like they just won
the big lottery.</i>

1298
01:18:17.751 --> 01:18:20.626
Diane:
<i>That was early</i>
<i>December of 2013.</i>

1299
01:18:20.709 --> 01:18:22.667
All the answers
came back right on.

1300
01:18:22.751 --> 01:18:24.500
We knew they had Jim.

1301
01:18:24.584 --> 01:18:26.042
<i>But by the end of December</i>

1302
01:18:26.125 --> 01:18:28.209
<i>they e-mailed us
back and said.</i>

1303
01:18:28.292 --> 01:18:31.709
"This is the last e-mail
you'll get from us"

1304
01:18:31.792 --> 01:18:33.000
and cut off
communication.

1305
01:18:35.125 --> 01:18:37.000
John. Sr.:
<i>The first year.</i>
<i>we just trusted</i>

1306
01:18:37.083 --> 01:18:38.918
<i>that the government
would have this in hand</i>

1307
01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:42.584
<i>and despite our lack
of information. et cetera.</i>

1308
01:18:42.667 --> 01:18:44.792
they had been
through this before
and they knew what to do.

1309
01:18:44.876 --> 01:18:47.626
and we were in good hands.

1310
01:18:47.709 --> 01:18:51.167
At the end of that year.
we realized that nothing
was being done

1311
01:18:51.250 --> 01:18:53.292
<i>and that we were really</i>

1312
01:18:53.375 --> 01:18:55.792
going to have to do
something on our own.

1313
01:19:05.167 --> 01:19:07.292
Phil:
<i>Diane was great</i>
<i>about meeting</i>

1314
01:19:07.375 --> 01:19:10.000
<i>with ambassadors
in Washington</i>

1315
01:19:10.083 --> 01:19:11.751
<i>from other countries</i>

1316
01:19:11.834 --> 01:19:15.500
<i>that might have
some influence.</i>

1317
01:19:15.584 --> 01:19:18.834
It turned out that
no one had any influence.

1318
01:19:18.918 --> 01:19:21.000
because the group
that had him

1319
01:19:21.083 --> 01:19:23.584
didn't listen to anybody.
but we didn't know that.

1320
01:19:23.667 --> 01:19:25.918
Man:
<i>It's kind of come as</i>
<i>a surprise to a lot of us.</i>

1321
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:28.417
<i>this group ISIS.
a group that we hadn't
really heard much about.</i>

1322
01:19:28.500 --> 01:19:30.167
<i>Who exactly are they?</i>

1323
01:19:30.250 --> 01:19:32.334
Woman:
<i>Well. It's a criminal</i>
<i>marauding gang.</i>

1324
01:19:32.417 --> 01:19:34.167
They come out
of the original.

1325
01:19:34.250 --> 01:19:38.042
<i>very brutal
Iraqi terror group.</i>

1326
01:19:38.125 --> 01:19:39.500
Michael:
<i>They're the worst.</i>

1327
01:19:39.584 --> 01:19:41.459
<i>They're the worst
of the worst.</i>

1328
01:19:41.542 --> 01:19:43.751
<i>Washington doesn't know
how to deal with them.</i>

1329
01:19:43.834 --> 01:19:45.083
How's this family
in New England

1330
01:19:45.167 --> 01:19:46.792
going to figure
that out. right?

1331
01:19:46.876 --> 01:19:50.626
<i>I mean you're dealing
with pure evil.</i>

1332
01:19:50.709 --> 01:19:55.209
<i>but a capable
and organized group.</i>

1333
01:20:49.667 --> 01:20:51.667
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1334
01:21:09.042 --> 01:21:11.375
Daniel:
<i>They were very tough</i>
<i>towards me. also.</i>

1335
01:21:11.459 --> 01:21:15.167
But no matter what.
they meant freedom for me.

1336
01:21:15.250 --> 01:21:18.626
because they were the ones
negotiating for me.

1337
01:21:18.709 --> 01:21:21.459
But James and John
were destroyed

1338
01:21:21.542 --> 01:21:23.792
<i>by the Beatles
in the beginning.</i>

1339
01:21:31.542 --> 01:21:33.959
Pierre:
<i>He had this ability</i>

1340
01:21:34.042 --> 01:21:36.083
<i>to escape the situation.</i>

1341
01:21:36.167 --> 01:21:40.626
<i>To enjoy. you know.
the sound of children</i>

1342
01:21:40.709 --> 01:21:42.292
<i>playing outside.</i>

1343
01:21:45.500 --> 01:21:49.959
<i>Or enjoyed the view
of just some sun</i>

1344
01:21:50.042 --> 01:21:52.292
<i>entering through
the window.</i>

1345
01:21:58.834 --> 01:22:01.125
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1346
01:22:17.584 --> 01:22:19.667
<i>( man chanting )</i>

1347
01:22:26.792 --> 01:22:28.792
Daniel:
<i>James converted</i>
<i>in the beginning</i>

1348
01:22:28.876 --> 01:22:30.584
<i>of his captivity.</i>

1349
01:22:33.042 --> 01:22:36.125
And I know that
was at the same period

1350
01:22:36.209 --> 01:22:39.334
as he were getting
really bad treatment.

1351
01:22:41.250 --> 01:22:43.792
<i>It gave a good routine.</i>

1352
01:22:43.876 --> 01:22:47.042
Normally. we have a tradition
of going into church
every Sunday.

1353
01:22:47.125 --> 01:22:48.792
But if you cannot do that.

1354
01:22:48.876 --> 01:22:50.959
you need another way
of feeling

1355
01:22:51.042 --> 01:22:53.876
that you are doing
something with your faith.

1356
01:22:55.459 --> 01:22:57.709
<i>You can call it
a surviving skill.</i>

1357
01:22:57.792 --> 01:22:59.584
<i>You can call it
just a way</i>

1358
01:22:59.667 --> 01:23:03.375
<i>of being interested
in another culture.</i>

1359
01:23:03.459 --> 01:23:05.042
What James used
to say to me

1360
01:23:05.125 --> 01:23:08.292
was that for him
God is the same.

1361
01:23:10.209 --> 01:23:12.042
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1362
01:23:38.792 --> 01:23:40.083
Diane:
<i>We had such</i>
<i>great Christmases</i>

1363
01:23:40.167 --> 01:23:41.626
<i>when the kids were little.</i>

1364
01:23:41.709 --> 01:23:44.876
<i>Just wonderful.
wonderful Christmases.</i>

1365
01:23:44.959 --> 01:23:46.834
And. you know.
as they got older

1366
01:23:46.918 --> 01:23:49.209
and Jim's
siblings married.

1367
01:23:49.292 --> 01:23:51.292
<i>they weren't always
able to be home.</i>

1368
01:23:51.375 --> 01:23:53.542
<i>But Jim was always home.</i>

1369
01:23:53.626 --> 01:23:57.751
Most of my memories of Jim
culminate around the holidays.

1370
01:23:57.834 --> 01:23:59.542
<i>You know. the playing
of the video games.</i>

1371
01:23:59.626 --> 01:24:02.626
<i>the board games.
the Ping-Pong tournaments.</i>

1372
01:24:02.709 --> 01:24:05.584
<i>the anything tournaments.</i>

1373
01:24:05.667 --> 01:24:08.459
( overlapping chatter )

1374
01:24:08.542 --> 01:24:10.876
John. Jr.:
<i>And you wake up</i>
<i>and you open the gifts.</i>

1375
01:24:10.959 --> 01:24:13.959
and you're like.
"Crap. Jim has my name.

1376
01:24:14.042 --> 01:24:16.167
<i>What's he going to
give me?"</i>

1377
01:24:16.250 --> 01:24:18.709
<i>( laughing )</i>

1378
01:24:18.792 --> 01:24:20.876
I shouldn't say that.

1379
01:24:22.042 --> 01:24:24.292
<i>It was good times.</i>

1380
01:24:24.375 --> 01:24:27.083
And then the last
Christmas he was home.

1381
01:24:27.167 --> 01:24:30.083
I remember him
rolling on the floor

1382
01:24:30.167 --> 01:24:32.334
<i>with Michael's son Matty.</i>

1383
01:24:32.417 --> 01:24:34.083
<i>He loved that kid.</i>

1384
01:24:35.959 --> 01:24:38.167
There's a million
Christmas stories. though.

1385
01:24:44.125 --> 01:24:45.584
Daniel:
<i>It's difficult for us</i>

1386
01:24:45.667 --> 01:24:48.000
<i>to celebrate Christmas
in any way.</i>

1387
01:24:48.083 --> 01:24:50.876
<i>We didn't have any presents
to give each other.</i>

1388
01:24:50.959 --> 01:24:56.000
So. we decided
to sit down in a circle.

1389
01:24:56.083 --> 01:25:00.459
<i>and then we had to say
something nice to each other.</i>

1390
01:25:00.542 --> 01:25:03.709
<i>And I remember that
I said to James that.</i>

1391
01:25:03.792 --> 01:25:05.500
<i>"James. the first time
I met you was.</i>

1392
01:25:05.584 --> 01:25:07.167
<i>you know.
in this prison.</i>

1393
01:25:07.250 --> 01:25:10.083
<i>and you looked
as confused</i>

1394
01:25:10.167 --> 01:25:12.876
<i>as if you were just
dumped down on the Earth</i>

1395
01:25:12.959 --> 01:25:14.250
<i>from the moon
or something.</i>

1396
01:25:14.334 --> 01:25:17.792
And you basically
destroyed my whole idea

1397
01:25:17.876 --> 01:25:22.375
of this great war journalist
James Wright Foley.

1398
01:25:22.459 --> 01:25:26.709
And then suddenly I find out
that you are very clumsy.

1399
01:25:26.792 --> 01:25:28.709
you're very bad at sports.

1400
01:25:30.459 --> 01:25:34.042
But then again. you--

1401
01:25:34.125 --> 01:25:36.042
you're the most--"

1402
01:25:36.125 --> 01:25:38.375
I think I said to him.

1403
01:25:38.459 --> 01:25:40.500
"You're the most
honest person.

1404
01:25:40.584 --> 01:25:45.334
There is no evil at all
to find in you. James.

1405
01:25:45.417 --> 01:25:48.834
You are pure good.
Sometimes too good.

1406
01:25:48.918 --> 01:25:52.584
And I'm really happy
that I've met you."

1407
01:25:57.042 --> 01:25:59.042
<i>That was
our Christmas night.</i>

1408
01:25:59.125 --> 01:26:01.500
<i>When I went to bed that night.
I really felt that I had</i>

1409
01:26:01.584 --> 01:26:04.209
<i>the best Christmas night
in my life.</i>

1410
01:26:19.000 --> 01:26:21.500
( door creaks )

1411
01:26:22.918 --> 01:26:25.542
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1412
01:26:34.709 --> 01:26:36.918
<i>( Pierre speaking )</i>

1413
01:26:48.834 --> 01:26:51.292
<i>( Nicolas speaking )</i>

1414
01:27:55.459 --> 01:27:57.626
Diane:
<i>So that's when I started</i>
<i>to get more frantic.</i>

1415
01:27:57.709 --> 01:27:59.792
I mean. I thought there's
got to be some way

1416
01:27:59.876 --> 01:28:03.209
to get the French
and the US to talk.

1417
01:28:03.292 --> 01:28:05.083
Didier.

1418
01:28:05.167 --> 01:28:07.876
This is
my husband John.

1419
01:28:07.959 --> 01:28:10.959
Diane:
<i>They were willing to share all</i>
<i>kinds of things about Jim--</i>

1420
01:28:11.042 --> 01:28:13.626
<i>personally. how he was.
what they did.</i>

1421
01:28:13.709 --> 01:28:15.500
So I was starting
to get all this hope.

1422
01:28:15.584 --> 01:28:19.042
<i>And they have
a hostage crisis
unit in Paris</i>

1423
01:28:19.125 --> 01:28:21.584
<i>and they were very generous
with their time.</i>

1424
01:28:21.667 --> 01:28:23.918
So it was so different than
what I was experiencing.

1425
01:28:24.000 --> 01:28:26.542
I was just kind of like.
"Jeez." you know?

1426
01:28:26.626 --> 01:28:28.125
Jim and the others--

1427
01:28:28.209 --> 01:28:29.709
our other Americans
are as important

1428
01:28:29.792 --> 01:28:31.209
as these guys.
aren't they?

1429
01:28:32.959 --> 01:28:37.125
<i>And it was the last
night I was in Paris</i>

1430
01:28:37.209 --> 01:28:39.167
<i>and I got a phone call
from John.</i>

1431
01:28:39.250 --> 01:28:44.000
<i>He said. "Diane.
we got another e-mail
from the captors."</i>

1432
01:28:44.083 --> 01:28:47.083
I thought. "Oh. great.
What did they say?"

1433
01:28:47.167 --> 01:28:50.709
<i>And that's when they
threatened to kill Jim.</i>

1434
01:28:55.167 --> 01:28:59.626
But me.
in my cluelessness.

1435
01:28:59.709 --> 01:29:03.417
I was just excited that
they had reached out to us.

1436
01:29:03.500 --> 01:29:07.375
<i>We had raised about
$1 million in pledges</i>

1437
01:29:07.459 --> 01:29:09.417
and so I was so excited
to hear from them.

1438
01:29:09.500 --> 01:29:12.042
because we thought.
"Now we can tell them
we have this money."

1439
01:29:12.125 --> 01:29:14.626
I was just so clueless.

1440
01:29:16.542 --> 01:29:19.751
Phil:
<i>We knew that paying</i>
<i>a ransom was illegal.</i>

1441
01:29:19.834 --> 01:29:24.375
and we also knew that it
wouldn't have stopped us.

1442
01:29:24.459 --> 01:29:28.042
<i>The Foleys were prepared
to mortgage their house</i>

1443
01:29:28.125 --> 01:29:32.542
<i>and do what needed to be done
to make a payment.</i>

1444
01:29:32.626 --> 01:29:35.375
I have a lot
of evolving thoughts

1445
01:29:35.459 --> 01:29:37.042
about this whole process

1446
01:29:37.125 --> 01:29:40.209
and what
the government didn't do

1447
01:29:40.292 --> 01:29:42.459
that it should have done.

1448
01:29:42.542 --> 01:29:46.417
I mean. if you just
look at the facts.

1449
01:29:46.500 --> 01:29:49.000
<i>there are 15 European
hostages</i>

1450
01:29:49.083 --> 01:29:53.042
<i>who are alive
and with their families
and friends</i>

1451
01:29:53.125 --> 01:29:54.834
<i>and loved ones today.</i>

1452
01:29:57.876 --> 01:30:00.751
I wish we had started
raising money sooner.

1453
01:30:02.542 --> 01:30:04.375
I wish we had negotiated.

1454
01:30:04.459 --> 01:30:07.042
<i>I wish it had
turned out differently.</i>

1455
01:31:02.292 --> 01:31:05.626
Mark:<i> In some sense.</i>
<i>I was okay with the fact</i>
<i>that he got captured.</i>

1456
01:31:05.709 --> 01:31:09.375
because I knew
he was doing what
he wanted to do.

1457
01:31:09.459 --> 01:31:13.334
And as a good family member
and as a good brother.

1458
01:31:13.417 --> 01:31:15.083
I need to understand that.

1459
01:31:15.167 --> 01:31:19.292
<i>My mom did a great job
in keeping faith</i>

1460
01:31:19.375 --> 01:31:21.459
<i>and working as hard
as she could.</i>

1461
01:31:21.542 --> 01:31:23.959
<i>but from the get-go
once it happened.</i>

1462
01:31:24.042 --> 01:31:26.334
I kind of felt like
he was already gone.

1463
01:31:32.083 --> 01:31:34.667
Daniel:
<i>So after a group</i>
<i>of the other hostages</i>

1464
01:31:34.751 --> 01:31:36.667
<i>was released.
the Beatles.</i>

1465
01:31:36.751 --> 01:31:39.459
<i>they came back
the day after.</i>

1466
01:31:39.542 --> 01:31:42.792
and they--

1467
01:31:42.876 --> 01:31:47.083
they beat the shit
out of James and I.

1468
01:31:48.709 --> 01:31:50.667
<i>I think it's called
a charley horse.</i>

1469
01:31:50.751 --> 01:31:54.918
<i>when you put your knee
into the legs of a person.</i>

1470
01:31:55.000 --> 01:31:58.334
<i>They did that
to me and James</i>

1471
01:31:58.417 --> 01:32:01.751
<i>while we had to sit
in stress positions.</i>

1472
01:32:01.834 --> 01:32:03.417
I don't know
how many they gave us.

1473
01:32:03.500 --> 01:32:06.250
but it just continued.

1474
01:32:06.334 --> 01:32:09.959
I never tried anything
that hurt so much
in my entire life.

1475
01:32:12.626 --> 01:32:15.167
<i>And then they just left.</i>

1476
01:32:17.125 --> 01:32:18.792
<i>I was just laying
there crying.</i>

1477
01:32:18.876 --> 01:32:22.667
<i>and I couldn't be
in my own body of pure pain.</i>

1478
01:32:22.751 --> 01:32:26.667
And James in the other
corner of the room.

1479
01:32:26.751 --> 01:32:29.792
I knew he got exactly
the same treatment as me.

1480
01:32:29.876 --> 01:32:32.083
but I couldn't hear it.

1481
01:32:32.167 --> 01:32:34.250
<i>He just--</i>

1482
01:32:34.334 --> 01:32:37.334
<i>I don't know
if he kept it inside
or how he did it.</i>

1483
01:32:37.417 --> 01:32:40.500
<i>but only a few minutes
after they left James.</i>

1484
01:32:40.584 --> 01:32:43.792
<i>he kind of looked up
and asked me if I was okay.</i>

1485
01:32:43.876 --> 01:32:46.125
I was like.
"Shut up. James."

1486
01:32:46.209 --> 01:32:48.751
I remember. I said.
"Shut up. James."

1487
01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:53.709
"Don't ask me
if I'm okay."

1488
01:32:53.792 --> 01:32:56.751
"Don't worry about me.
Worry about yourself."

1489
01:32:58.876 --> 01:33:00.751
<i>Yeah. we just laid there</i>

1490
01:33:00.834 --> 01:33:04.000
<i>until the pain started
to disappear a bit.</i>

1491
01:33:05.918 --> 01:33:09.125
<i>Two or three days after.
they came in again.</i>

1492
01:33:09.209 --> 01:33:12.000
<i>This time. the Beatles
just told us that.</i>

1493
01:33:12.083 --> 01:33:14.167
"Now. guys.
everything's changed."

1494
01:33:14.250 --> 01:33:18.292
<i>And they took all
our food. our games.</i>

1495
01:33:18.375 --> 01:33:19.918
<i>most of the blankets.</i>

1496
01:33:22.250 --> 01:33:25.667
<i>They started to use
police clubs.</i>

1497
01:33:25.751 --> 01:33:27.167
<i>So. every time
we went to the toilet.</i>

1498
01:33:27.250 --> 01:33:29.918
<i>we got beatings
with a stick.</i>

1499
01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:34.292
<i>We were so scared.
We lost all hope.</i>

1500
01:33:34.375 --> 01:33:38.334
<i>No matter what
came through that door.
it was evil.</i>

1501
01:33:38.417 --> 01:33:41.209
<i>Those 14 days
before I got released</i>

1502
01:33:41.292 --> 01:33:44.042
was the absolute
worst times.

1503
01:33:50.000 --> 01:33:52.209
<i>When people started
to get released.</i>

1504
01:33:52.292 --> 01:33:55.042
<i>we decided
to send out letters</i>

1505
01:33:55.125 --> 01:33:58.542
<i>with the person
who was released.</i>

1506
01:33:58.626 --> 01:34:00.209
<i>But James. he didn't.</i>

1507
01:34:00.292 --> 01:34:02.459
He didn't want
to bother any of us.

1508
01:34:02.542 --> 01:34:07.209
<i>And I remember one day that
I saw that John and James</i>

1509
01:34:07.292 --> 01:34:10.959
<i>had been talking
for some time in the corner.</i>

1510
01:34:11.042 --> 01:34:13.167
<i>And after that. John.
he came over to me and said.</i>

1511
01:34:13.250 --> 01:34:15.417
<i>"Daniel. James. he wants
to ask you something."</i>

1512
01:34:15.500 --> 01:34:17.626
"Okay. Okay.
What's up. James?"

1513
01:34:17.709 --> 01:34:20.542
And he's like.
"Oh. uh. it's just if--

1514
01:34:20.626 --> 01:34:23.000
if you want to carry out
a message or something."

1515
01:34:23.083 --> 01:34:25.459
He was very--
he said it fast.

1516
01:34:25.542 --> 01:34:28.125
like he didn't
want to bother me.

1517
01:34:30.125 --> 01:34:33.125
<i>There's many
ways of dealing with
a situation like this.</i>

1518
01:34:33.209 --> 01:34:37.751
<i>And James.
I think one of the reasons
why he remained so strong</i>

1519
01:34:37.834 --> 01:34:41.918
was because
he managed to think about
all the good things.

1520
01:34:42.000 --> 01:34:46.000
<i>He saw the light
instead of the dark spots.</i>

1521
01:34:46.083 --> 01:34:50.334
<i>where a guy like John.
he was much more realistic.</i>

1522
01:34:50.417 --> 01:34:52.792
<i>He knew when it was bad.</i>

1523
01:34:56.918 --> 01:34:58.834
<i>But I couldn't
bring out the letter.</i>

1524
01:34:58.918 --> 01:35:01.918
<i>I was too afraid
after these 14 days.</i>

1525
01:35:02.000 --> 01:35:05.709
<i>so I decided
just to memorize it
as fast as I could.</i>

1526
01:35:05.792 --> 01:35:07.792
So I started waiting.

1527
01:35:07.876 --> 01:35:10.083
I knew they will
come in the morning
to pick me up.

1528
01:35:10.167 --> 01:35:12.751
<i>One day went.</i>

1529
01:35:12.834 --> 01:35:15.083
<i>Two days went.</i>

1530
01:35:15.167 --> 01:35:18.834
<i>And then fourth day.
fifth day.</i>

1531
01:35:18.918 --> 01:35:20.626
Shit. man.
Then I woke up there

1532
01:35:20.709 --> 01:35:23.959
the sixth day in the morning.
and I couldn't sleep.

1533
01:35:24.042 --> 01:35:26.042
I couldn't sleep all night
because I was so afraid

1534
01:35:26.125 --> 01:35:28.626
<i>of what if they are
playing a trick on me.</i>

1535
01:35:30.417 --> 01:35:32.125
<i>And James.
he walked over to me.</i>

1536
01:35:32.209 --> 01:35:34.292
<i>and he sat down
right next to me</i>

1537
01:35:34.375 --> 01:35:36.250
<i>and he said.
"Are you okay. Daniel?"</i>

1538
01:35:36.334 --> 01:35:38.709
I said. "Yeah."

1539
01:35:38.792 --> 01:35:40.417
Then I just couldn't
hold it back.

1540
01:35:40.500 --> 01:35:44.667
and I said. "Fuck. man.
I am really. really scared.

1541
01:35:44.751 --> 01:35:46.918
I'm really. really. really.
really scared right now.

1542
01:35:47.000 --> 01:35:49.083
I don't know what to do.
what to think. I--"

1543
01:35:49.167 --> 01:35:51.584
<i>And he said.
"Daniel. calm down.</i>

1544
01:35:51.667 --> 01:35:53.125
<i>Calm down.</i>

1545
01:35:53.209 --> 01:35:54.959
<i>Everything will be fine.</i>

1546
01:35:55.042 --> 01:35:56.626
<i>You are going home.</i>

1547
01:35:56.709 --> 01:36:00.000
<i>They will come in
a few hours or tomorrow.</i>

1548
01:36:00.083 --> 01:36:01.667
<i>but one thing
is sure. Daniel.</i>

1549
01:36:01.751 --> 01:36:03.834
<i>You will go home."</i>

1550
01:36:03.918 --> 01:36:05.667
<i>Once again.
it was a real feeling.</i>

1551
01:36:05.751 --> 01:36:07.375
because I was
sitting there crying

1552
01:36:07.459 --> 01:36:11.209
and making a scene
in front of James.

1553
01:36:11.292 --> 01:36:13.083
and I was about to go home

1554
01:36:13.167 --> 01:36:15.959
and. James. he didn't have
anything to look forward to.

1555
01:36:17.500 --> 01:36:20.417
<i>James. he went back to
his side of the room.</i>

1556
01:36:24.584 --> 01:36:28.042
10 minutes after.
they knocked on the door.

1557
01:36:28.125 --> 01:36:30.626
they came in
and asked me to put
a blanket over my head

1558
01:36:30.709 --> 01:36:32.751
and follow them.

1559
01:36:36.417 --> 01:36:39.125
<i>That was
the last time I--</i>

1560
01:36:39.209 --> 01:36:40.709
<i>I saw James.</i>

1561
01:37:02.083 --> 01:37:04.042
I call on my friends.
family. and loved ones

1562
01:37:04.125 --> 01:37:08.042
to rise up
against my real killers.
the US government.

1563
01:37:08.125 --> 01:37:09.918
For what will happen to me

1564
01:37:10.000 --> 01:37:13.459
is only a result
of their complacency
and criminality.

1565
01:37:13.542 --> 01:37:15.792
I wish I had more time.

1566
01:37:15.876 --> 01:37:17.334
I wish I could have
the hope of freedom

1567
01:37:17.417 --> 01:37:19.417
and seeing my family
once again.

1568
01:37:19.500 --> 01:37:21.250
but that ship has sailed.

1569
01:37:23.209 --> 01:37:26.834
Michael:
<i>I hadn't heard Jim's voice</i>
<i>in two years.</i>

1570
01:37:29.250 --> 01:37:33.042
I guess you can see it
in his neck. in his face--

1571
01:37:33.125 --> 01:37:36.792
<i>just this strength
he has at that moment.</i>

1572
01:37:38.375 --> 01:37:41.250
And I think of myself.

1573
01:37:41.334 --> 01:37:44.209
I'd be calling for
my mother or something.

1574
01:37:44.292 --> 01:37:48.250
Just the strength
he had and I--

1575
01:37:48.334 --> 01:37:52.417
I wanted to feel
what he felt.

1576
01:37:52.500 --> 01:37:54.709
was the reason
I watched it.

1577
01:37:58.542 --> 01:38:01.584
I kept getting
messages saying.
"John. are you okay?

1578
01:38:01.667 --> 01:38:04.667
John. are you okay about
what your brother said?"

1579
01:38:04.751 --> 01:38:07.042
I'm like. "What are you
talking about?"

1580
01:38:07.125 --> 01:38:08.918
I call on my brother John.

1581
01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:12.459
who is a member
of the US Air Force.

1582
01:38:12.542 --> 01:38:15.125
Think about
what you are doing.

1583
01:38:15.209 --> 01:38:17.709
Think about
the lives you destroy.

1584
01:38:17.792 --> 01:38:19.792
including those
of your own family.

1585
01:38:22.375 --> 01:38:24.250
Even though he was
reading the script.

1586
01:38:24.334 --> 01:38:28.751
he seemed defiant
to the end.

1587
01:38:30.417 --> 01:38:31.626
I mean. for sure.

1588
01:38:31.709 --> 01:38:36.417
I still have some guilt
regarding me and--

1589
01:38:36.500 --> 01:38:40.626
just me and.
unfortunately.
my profession.

1590
01:38:40.709 --> 01:38:44.417
but I'm sure that
he endured torture.

1591
01:38:45.876 --> 01:38:47.500
Interestingly.
John and I have--

1592
01:38:47.584 --> 01:38:51.709
we've argued a lot
about things since that.

1593
01:38:51.792 --> 01:38:54.334
but we've been
communicating a lot more.

1594
01:38:54.417 --> 01:38:57.250
<i>We've come a lot closer
as a result of it.</i>

1595
01:38:57.334 --> 01:39:00.292
<i>I think Jim
would have loved that.</i>

1596
01:39:00.375 --> 01:39:02.334
<i>You know Jim
as well as I do.</i>

1597
01:39:02.417 --> 01:39:04.667
He wouldn't
have said those things
if there wasn't someone else

1598
01:39:04.751 --> 01:39:06.751
that was going to be harmed
as a result of it.

1599
01:39:06.834 --> 01:39:08.626
He never cared
about himself.

1600
01:39:10.918 --> 01:39:13.083
Katie:
<i>My only thought</i>
<i>when I found out about Jim</i>

1601
01:39:13.167 --> 01:39:14.959
<i>was how am I
going to get home?</i>

1602
01:39:15.042 --> 01:39:17.542
<i>I had missed
the initial chaos</i>

1603
01:39:17.626 --> 01:39:19.083
<i>of when it all happened.</i>

1604
01:39:19.167 --> 01:39:23.000
<i>but slowly we all
kind of came together.</i>

1605
01:39:23.083 --> 01:39:26.375
We literally chose
the smallest room possible
in our house.

1606
01:39:26.459 --> 01:39:28.792
but everybody was just
kind of huddled together.

1607
01:39:30.792 --> 01:39:33.542
<i>Jim always found a way
to bring people together.</i>

1608
01:39:33.626 --> 01:39:35.667
Always.

1609
01:39:38.459 --> 01:39:40.542
Diane:
<i>I really don't think</i>

1610
01:39:40.626 --> 01:39:43.083
<i>I came to know
Jim as a man.</i>

1611
01:39:43.167 --> 01:39:45.209
<i>as an adult.</i>

1612
01:39:45.292 --> 01:39:47.667
I came to know him
through his friends.

1613
01:39:50.918 --> 01:39:52.751
<i>It's hard as a mother.</i>

1614
01:39:52.834 --> 01:39:56.209
<i>You know. they're always
your children somehow.</i>

1615
01:39:58.083 --> 01:40:00.834
And it's hard to see
that they're adults

1616
01:40:00.918 --> 01:40:04.584
with their own gifts
and their own way
in the world.

1617
01:40:07.250 --> 01:40:10.083
John Jr.:
<i>The day before</i>
<i>his memorial.</i>

1618
01:40:10.167 --> 01:40:13.876
I was like. "Shit."

1619
01:40:13.959 --> 01:40:16.709
I was like.
"Now I get it.

1620
01:40:16.792 --> 01:40:19.125
Now I get it."

1621
01:40:19.209 --> 01:40:23.876
Jokingly. I was always like.
you know. "Jim. get a job.

1622
01:40:23.959 --> 01:40:27.626
<i>Jim. save for retirement."</i>

1623
01:40:27.709 --> 01:40:29.751
<i>What I didn't recognize.</i>

1624
01:40:29.834 --> 01:40:31.542
<i>he was really trying
to teach me.</i>

1625
01:40:31.626 --> 01:40:35.834
"John. you need to look
outside of yourself.

1626
01:40:35.918 --> 01:40:40.876
John. it's not about
physical or monetary things.

1627
01:40:40.959 --> 01:40:43.459
<i>It's about how you act.</i>

1628
01:40:43.542 --> 01:40:47.459
<i>It's who you teach.
who you mentor.</i>

1629
01:40:47.542 --> 01:40:49.959
<i>Who's going
to remember you?</i>

1630
01:40:50.042 --> 01:40:51.959
<i>What are they going
to remember about you?"</i>

1631
01:40:56.042 --> 01:40:57.876
John. Sr.:
<i>Jimmy was included</i>

1632
01:40:57.959 --> 01:40:59.459
<i>in the long line
of journalists</i>

1633
01:40:59.542 --> 01:41:02.125
<i>who gave their lives
to tell the truth.</i>

1634
01:41:02.209 --> 01:41:03.626
We were just totally humbled

1635
01:41:03.709 --> 01:41:06.417
by the fact that
the committee in Bayeux

1636
01:41:06.500 --> 01:41:09.876
<i>would nominate him
to be included</i>

1637
01:41:09.959 --> 01:41:11.500
<i>on one of the markers</i>

1638
01:41:11.584 --> 01:41:14.918
<i>with 2.000 other deceased
journalists.</i>

1639
01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:18.125
<i>It was a very
important moment.</i>

1640
01:41:20.584 --> 01:41:22.250
<i>This was Jim.</i>

1641
01:41:22.334 --> 01:41:26.250
and it made me
feel very warm inside

1642
01:41:26.334 --> 01:41:30.375
that he was accepted
as one of them.

1643
01:41:32.000 --> 01:41:33.834
<i>( Didier speaking )</i>

1644
01:41:54.459 --> 01:41:56.125
John. Sr.:
<i>And that's the difference</i>
<i>with Jim.</i>

1645
01:41:56.209 --> 01:41:58.459
<i>He was just
a you and a me--</i>

1646
01:41:58.542 --> 01:42:02.209
a friendly guy--
until tested.

1647
01:42:04.167 --> 01:42:06.542
Where that came from--
I don't know. Brian.

1648
01:42:11.000 --> 01:42:14.709
Because you just can't grit
something like that out.

1649
01:42:14.792 --> 01:42:19.209
You have to have
an inner strength

1650
01:42:19.292 --> 01:42:20.250
to do that.

1651
01:42:22.417 --> 01:42:25.209
<i>We discovered Jim
just like the world did.</i>

1652
01:42:28.042 --> 01:42:30.042
<i>He was truly
a man for others.</i>

1653
01:42:34.375 --> 01:42:36.876
Nicole:
<i>Witnessing Jim's murder</i>
<i>this publicly.</i>

1654
01:42:36.959 --> 01:42:39.167
<i>it sends a message
to all of us.</i>

1655
01:42:39.250 --> 01:42:42.959
And my. I guess.
response to that

1656
01:42:43.042 --> 01:42:45.959
is having lost
so many friends

1657
01:42:46.042 --> 01:42:48.334
<i>and knowing that people</i>

1658
01:42:48.417 --> 01:42:51.375
<i>are purposely out
for journalists now.</i>

1659
01:42:51.459 --> 01:42:53.375
<i>I mean.
we have to fight back</i>

1660
01:42:53.459 --> 01:42:58.083
<i>with our pictures
and our words.</i>

1661
01:42:58.167 --> 01:43:00.250
I don't want to
let them win.

1662
01:43:02.250 --> 01:43:06.626
Clare:
<i>The extent to which the media</i>
<i>coverage took off.</i>

1663
01:43:06.709 --> 01:43:09.626
it was just staggering.
It was absolutely
staggering.

1664
01:43:09.709 --> 01:43:13.375
<i>Something like
94% of Americans</i>

1665
01:43:13.459 --> 01:43:16.876
<i>were aware of Jim's death
and how he died.</i>

1666
01:43:16.959 --> 01:43:20.375
It's the event with
the second most recognition

1667
01:43:20.459 --> 01:43:23.167
in recent American history
after 9/11.

1668
01:43:23.250 --> 01:43:25.083
He would have been
horrified by that.

1669
01:43:25.167 --> 01:43:28.000
I mean. he was there to talk
about the Syrian people

1670
01:43:28.083 --> 01:43:30.083
<i>and this is the takeaway.</i>

1671
01:43:33.125 --> 01:43:35.334
Brian:
<i>What is your response</i>
<i>to people</i>

1672
01:43:35.417 --> 01:43:38.542
<i>who would say he shouldn't
have been in there?</i>

1673
01:43:38.626 --> 01:43:41.959
Clare:
<i>My response is do you</i>
<i>read the newspaper?</i>

1674
01:43:42.042 --> 01:43:45.042
<i>Do you watch TV?
You're depending on someone</i>

1675
01:43:45.125 --> 01:43:47.542
<i>to bring you
that information.</i>

1676
01:43:47.626 --> 01:43:50.459
<i>If you care about
what's going on in Syria.</i>

1677
01:43:50.542 --> 01:43:52.667
<i>you don't have
the right to be like.</i>

1678
01:43:52.751 --> 01:43:53.959
"Oh. why's he there?"

1679
01:43:54.042 --> 01:43:55.542
How do you even
know what there is?

1680
01:43:55.626 --> 01:43:57.542
Because he told you.

1681
01:44:02.292 --> 01:44:03.626
Pierre:
<i>I remember the video.</i>

1682
01:44:03.709 --> 01:44:06.000
<i>and there was a desert.</i>

1683
01:44:07.751 --> 01:44:09.751
<i>And in the background</i>

1684
01:44:09.834 --> 01:44:12.375
<i>you could see that
the desert stopped.</i>

1685
01:44:12.459 --> 01:44:16.584
<i>and there was all
the Valley of Euphrates.</i>

1686
01:44:16.667 --> 01:44:20.959
So. according to what
I know from James.

1687
01:44:21.042 --> 01:44:23.918
I'm. like. 100% sure

1688
01:44:24.000 --> 01:44:27.918
<i>that even though
he was convinced he would
be killed.</i>

1689
01:44:28.000 --> 01:44:29.751
<i>he enjoyed the view.</i>

1690
01:44:33.792 --> 01:44:36.250
Nicolas:
<i>He died as a free man.</i>

1691
01:44:36.334 --> 01:44:38.876
<i>This is not the death
of a hostage.</i>

1692
01:44:38.959 --> 01:44:41.125
And this is the difference.
eventually.

1693
01:44:41.209 --> 01:44:44.667
between Jim and myself.

1694
01:44:44.751 --> 01:44:48.918
I ended up being released.

1695
01:44:49.000 --> 01:44:51.709
but he ended up free.

1696
01:44:57.292 --> 01:45:00.250
Daniel:
<i>And when we came</i>
<i>to James' funeral.</i>

1697
01:45:00.334 --> 01:45:03.876
everything started to become
real again in a way.

1698
01:45:03.959 --> 01:45:06.250
The fact that I managed
to say good-bye

1699
01:45:06.334 --> 01:45:08.584
<i>to James together
with all his loved ones</i>

1700
01:45:08.667 --> 01:45:10.042
<i>was really powerful.</i>

1701
01:45:10.125 --> 01:45:12.792
<i>It reminded me
how important it was that--</i>

1702
01:45:12.876 --> 01:45:16.626
that. James. he gave
that letter to me.

1703
01:45:24.918 --> 01:45:26.459
Michael:
<i>"Dear family and friends.</i>

1704
01:45:26.542 --> 01:45:29.542
<i>I remember going to
the mall with Dad.</i>

1705
01:45:29.626 --> 01:45:32.083
<i>a very long
bike ride with Mom.</i>

1706
01:45:32.167 --> 01:45:34.083
<i>I remember so many
great family times</i>

1707
01:45:34.167 --> 01:45:36.667
<i>that take me away
from this prison.</i>

1708
01:45:36.751 --> 01:45:39.000
<i>Dreams of family
and friends take me away</i>

1709
01:45:39.083 --> 01:45:41.250
<i>and happiness
fills my heart.</i>

1710
01:45:43.375 --> 01:45:46.042
<i>I know you're thinking
of me and praying for me.</i>

1711
01:45:46.125 --> 01:45:48.375
<i>and I am so thankful.</i>

1712
01:45:48.459 --> 01:45:50.500
<i>I feel you all.
especially when I pray.</i>

1713
01:45:50.584 --> 01:45:53.584
<i>I pray for you
to stay strong
and to believe.</i>

1714
01:45:53.667 --> 01:45:54.959
<i>I really feel
I can touch you.</i>

1715
01:45:55.042 --> 01:45:57.042
<i>even in this darkness.
when I pray.</i>

1716
01:46:02.709 --> 01:46:04.959
<i>I think a lot about
my brothers and sister.</i>

1717
01:46:05.042 --> 01:46:08.375
<i>I remember playing werewolf
in the dark with Michael</i>

1718
01:46:08.459 --> 01:46:10.125
<i>and so many other
adventures.</i>

1719
01:46:12.250 --> 01:46:15.876
<i>I think of chasing
Matty and T. around
the kitchen counter.</i>

1720
01:46:15.959 --> 01:46:18.042
<i>It makes me happy
to think of them.</i>

1721
01:46:18.125 --> 01:46:20.292
<i>If there is any money
left in my bank account.</i>

1722
01:46:20.375 --> 01:46:23.417
<i>I want it to go
to Michael and Matthew.</i>

1723
01:46:23.500 --> 01:46:25.959
<i>I am so proud of you.
Michael.</i>

1724
01:46:26.042 --> 01:46:28.667
<i>and thankful to you
for happy childhood memories</i>

1725
01:46:28.751 --> 01:46:31.083
<i>and to you and Kristie
for happy adult ones.</i>

1726
01:46:35.375 --> 01:46:36.918
<i>And. Big John.</i>

1727
01:46:37.000 --> 01:46:39.626
<i>how I enjoyed visiting you
and Cress in Germany.</i>

1728
01:46:39.709 --> 01:46:41.500
<i>Thank you
for welcoming me.</i>

1729
01:46:41.584 --> 01:46:42.959
<i>I think a lot about RoRo</i>

1730
01:46:43.042 --> 01:46:45.167
<i>and try to imagine
what Jack is like.</i>

1731
01:46:45.250 --> 01:46:48.167
<i>I hope he has RoRo's
personality.</i>

1732
01:46:48.250 --> 01:46:49.834
Priest:
<i>So. Mark and Kasey.</i>

1733
01:46:49.918 --> 01:46:53.250
<i>what name have you
given your son?</i>

1734
01:46:53.334 --> 01:46:55.042
James Michael Foley.

1735
01:46:55.125 --> 01:46:57.500
<i>James Michael Foley.</i>

1736
01:46:57.584 --> 01:47:01.959
Michael:
<i>And. Mark. so proud</i>
<i>of you. too. bro.</i>

1737
01:47:02.042 --> 01:47:03.792
<i>I think of you
on the West Coast</i>

1738
01:47:03.876 --> 01:47:05.834
<i>and hope you're doing some
snowboarding and camping.</i>

1739
01:47:05.918 --> 01:47:07.417
<i>I especially remember us</i>

1740
01:47:07.500 --> 01:47:09.459
<i>going to the comedy club
in Boston together</i>

1741
01:47:09.542 --> 01:47:12.459
<i>and our big hug after.</i>

1742
01:47:12.542 --> 01:47:14.542
<i>The special moments
keep me hopeful.</i>

1743
01:47:17.918 --> 01:47:20.459
<i>Katie. so very
proud of you.</i>

1744
01:47:20.542 --> 01:47:23.042
<i>You are the strongest
and best of us all.</i>

1745
01:47:23.125 --> 01:47:24.667
<i>I think of you
working so hard</i>

1746
01:47:24.751 --> 01:47:27.000
<i>helping people
as a nurse.</i>

1747
01:47:27.083 --> 01:47:29.626
<i>I pray I can come
to your wedding.</i>

1748
01:47:29.709 --> 01:47:31.500
<i>Now I'm sounding
like Grammy.</i>

1749
01:47:33.876 --> 01:47:35.792
<i>Grammy. please
take your medicine.</i>

1750
01:47:35.876 --> 01:47:38.500
<i>Take walks
and keep dancing.</i>

1751
01:47:38.584 --> 01:47:41.125
<i>I plan to take you out to
Margaritas when I get home.</i>

1752
01:47:41.209 --> 01:47:44.042
<i>Stay strong. because I am
going to need your help</i>

1753
01:47:44.125 --> 01:47:47.375
<i>to reclaim my life.
Jim."</i>

1754
01:48:03.959 --> 01:48:06.876
<i>♪ If I should
close my eyes ♪</i>

1755
01:48:06.959 --> 01:48:10.125
<i>♪ That my soul can see ♪</i>

1756
01:48:10.209 --> 01:48:13.042
<i>♪ There's a place
at the table ♪</i>

1757
01:48:13.125 --> 01:48:16.334
<i>♪ That you save for me ♪</i>

1758
01:48:16.417 --> 01:48:19.334
<i>♪ So many thousand miles ♪</i>

1759
01:48:19.417 --> 01:48:22.918
<i>♪ Over land and sea ♪</i>

1760
01:48:23.000 --> 01:48:25.834
<i>♪ I hope true. dear ♪</i>

1761
01:48:25.918 --> 01:48:29.876
<i>♪ That you hear my prayer ♪</i>

1762
01:48:29.959 --> 01:48:36.375
<i>♪ And somehow I'll be there ♪</i>

1763
01:48:38.334 --> 01:48:41.959
<i>♪ There's but
a concrete floor ♪</i>

1764
01:48:42.042 --> 01:48:44.876
<i>♪ Where my head will lay ♪</i>

1765
01:48:44.959 --> 01:48:47.751
<i>♪ And though the walls
of this prison ♪</i>

1766
01:48:47.834 --> 01:48:50.959
<i>♪ Are as cold as clay ♪</i>

1767
01:48:51.042 --> 01:48:53.918
<i>♪ But there's a shaft
of light ♪</i>

1768
01:48:54.000 --> 01:48:57.626
<i>♪ Where I count my days ♪</i>

1769
01:48:57.709 --> 01:49:01.125
<i>♪ So don't despair ♪</i>

1770
01:49:01.209 --> 01:49:05.751
<i>♪ Of the empty chair ♪</i>

1771
01:49:05.834 --> 01:49:10.876
<i>♪ And somehow
I'll be there ♪</i>

1772
01:49:12.459 --> 01:49:15.459
<i>♪ Some days I'm strong ♪</i>

1773
01:49:15.542 --> 01:49:18.375
<i>♪ Some days I'm weak ♪</i>

1774
01:49:18.459 --> 01:49:23.959
<i>♪ On days when I'm broken.
I can barely speak ♪</i>

1775
